window.ECHS_UNIT1_V5_CONTENT={"1.2":{"number":"1.2","title":"Arithmetic Sequences and Series","subtitle":"Model constant additive change, move fluently between representations, and accumulate terms with justified formulas.","objectives":["Distinguish a sequence, a term, a series and a partial sum using precise notation.","Recognize arithmetic structure from constant first differences in tables, lists and contexts.","Use recursive and explicit forms, including inverse problems for the index or parameters.","Calculate and justify finite arithmetic sums and sigma expressions.","Evaluate when a constant-additive model is useful and when its assumptions fail."],"vocab":["sequence","term","index","series","partial sum","sigma notation","arithmetic sequence","common difference","recursive rule","explicit rule"],"technology":"Use sequence or spreadsheet mode to generate terms and cumulative sums. Before trusting the output, verify the first term, one difference, the indexing convention and the units.","source_basis":["Nikolaidis Topic 1A — sequences, series, sigma notation and arithmetic sequences","Supplied MAI 1.4–1.5 arithmetic-sequence exercise set","Pearson AI SL — sequences and series teaching structure"],"syllabus_focus":"Unit 1 — arithmetic sequences and series, explicit and recursive forms, finite sums and applications.","slides":[{"section":"Launch","title":"1.2 · Arithmetic Sequences and Series","html":"<div class=\"u1-cover\"><div><div class=\"u1-kicker\">IB DP Mathematics · Applications and Interpretation SL</div><h1><span>1.2</span>Arithmetic Sequences and Series</h1><p class=\"u1-cover-lead\">Model constant additive change, move fluently between representations, and accumulate terms with justified formulas.</p><div class=\"u1-pill-row\"><span>36 purposeful screens</span><span>52 Practice Studio questions</span><span>14-question quiz</span><span>3 IB tasks</span></div><div class=\"u1-central-idea\"><b>Central idea</b><p>Arithmetic structure is not “a list that looks regular”; it is the invariant first difference that connects term rules, graphs, sums and contextual predictions.</p></div><button class=\"route-jump u1-start\" data-go=\"learn\" data-cover-next=\"1\">Begin lesson →</button></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Constant additive change\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Constant additive change</text>\n    <line x1=\"70\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M80.0,240.0 L172.0,212.0 L264.0,184.0 L356.0,156.0 L448.0,128.0 L540.0,100.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#24557d\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 7\"/><circle cx=\"80.0\" cy=\"240.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">1</text><circle cx=\"172.0\" cy=\"212.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"172.0\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">2</text><circle cx=\"264.0\" cy=\"184.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><text x=\"264.0\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">3</text><circle cx=\"356.0\" cy=\"156.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/><text x=\"356.0\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">4</text><circle cx=\"448.0\" cy=\"128.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"448.0\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">5</text><circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"100.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">6</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">equal index steps · equal vertical differences</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"cover","eyebrow":"Unit 1 · Number and Algebra"},{"section":"Launch","title":"A question worth investigating","html":"<div class=\"u1-launch\"><div><blockquote>When does adding the same amount repeatedly create a trustworthy model—and how can we detect the moment it stops being trustworthy?</blockquote><p>A school theatre adds three seats in every new row. The rule looks simple, but planning requires distinguishing a row count from a total seat count and deciding whether aisles eventually break the pattern.</p><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"launch-inquiry\" aria-label=\"Initial inquiry response\"></textarea></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Rows in a theatre\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Rows in a theatre</text>\n    <defs><marker id=\"tarr\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L7,3 z\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><circle cx=\"70.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">row 1</text><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">18</text><path d=\"M84.0 155 Q150.0 90 216.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"150.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">+3</text><circle cx=\"230.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><text x=\"230.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">row 2</text><text x=\"230.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">21</text><path d=\"M244.0 155 Q310.0 90 376.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">+3</text><circle cx=\"390.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><text x=\"390.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">row 3</text><text x=\"390.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">24</text><path d=\"M404.0 155 Q470.0 90 536.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"470.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">+3</text><circle cx=\"550.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"550.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">row 4</text><text x=\"550.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">27</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">term values are not cumulative totals</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"inquiry","eyebrow":"Think · discuss · revisit"},{"section":"Learning route","title":"Learning intentions and evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-goals\"><div class=\"u1-goal-grid\"><article><b>1</b><p>Distinguish a sequence, a term, a series and a partial sum using precise notation.</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Recognize arithmetic structure from constant first differences in tables, lists and contexts.</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Use recursive and explicit forms, including inverse problems for the index or parameters.</p></article><article><b>4</b><p>Calculate and justify finite arithmetic sums and sigma expressions.</p></article><article><b>5</b><p>Evaluate when a constant-additive model is useful and when its assumptions fail.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-evidence\"><h3>Evidence of understanding</h3><ul><li>Explain why the exponent or coefficient is \\(n-1\\), not merely quote a formula.</li><li>Recover \\(u_1\\) and \\(d\\) from separated terms and verify both conditions.</li><li>Interpret \\(u_n\\) and \\(S_n\\) with units in context.</li><li>Enforce that a term number is a positive integer and check a threshold on both sides.</li></ul></div></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Know what success looks like"},{"section":"Diagnostic","title":"Try before instruction","html":"<div class=\"u1-diagnostic\"><div><span>Diagnostic</span><p>A training plan records distances \\(18,25,32,39,\\ldots\\) km at equal weekly intervals. What remains constant, what is the tenth value, and how is the tenth value different from the total distance over ten weeks?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"diagnostic\"></textarea><details><summary>Diagnostic lens</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>A strong response identifies \\(d=7\\), uses nine jumps to obtain \\(u_{10}=81\\), and distinguishes a term from the partial sum \\(S_{10}\\).</p></div></details></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Term versus sum\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Term versus sum</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">u₁₀</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">81 km</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">the tenth weekly distance</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">S₁₀</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">495 km</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">the total across ten weeks</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">same sequence · different mathematical question</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Use errors as information"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Sequence, term, series and sigma notation","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>A sequence is an ordered list \\(u_1,u_2,\\ldots\\). A series adds selected terms, and \\(S_n\\) means the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms. Sigma notation states the index rule and bounds compactly. The index is discrete, so a sequence graph consists of isolated points.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[S_n=u_1+u_2+\\cdots+u_n=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}u_k\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Say “the tenth term” for \\(u_{10}\\) and “the sum of the first ten terms” for \\(S_{10}\\).</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Sequence, term, series and sigma notation\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Sequence, term, series and sigma notation</text>\n    <line x1=\"70\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M80.0,240.0 L195.0,205.0 L310.0,170.0 L425.0,135.0 L540.0,100.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#24557d\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 7\"/><circle cx=\"80.0\" cy=\"240.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">1</text><circle cx=\"195.0\" cy=\"205.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"195.0\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">2</text><circle cx=\"310.0\" cy=\"170.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">3</text><circle cx=\"425.0\" cy=\"135.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/><text x=\"425.0\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">4</text><circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"100.0\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">5</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">an ordered list lives on integer indices</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 1 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Worked example · read notation before calculating","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>For \\(4,9,14,19,24,\\ldots\\), state \\(u_3\\), \\(S_3\\), and expand \\(\\sum_{k=2}^{5}u_k\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Read the index</span><p>\\(u_3=14\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Accumulate the requested first terms</span><p>\\(S_3=4+9+14=27\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Respect the sigma limits</span><p>\\(\\sum_{k=2}^{5}u_k=9+14+19+24=66\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Notation decides which quantities are selected before any arithmetic begins.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>For \\(u_n=7-2n\\), list \\(u_1,u_2,u_3,u_4\\) and evaluate \\(\\sum_{n=1}^{4}u_n\\).</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-1\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(u_1=5,u_2=3,u_3=1,u_4=-1\\), so the sum is \\(5+3+1-1=8\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Substituting the index values gives \\(5,3,1,-1\\). The sigma expression asks for all four of these terms, not for the fourth term alone, so its value is \\(8\\).</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">How do the units or meaning of a term differ from the units or meaning of a partial sum?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“A sequence and its series are the same object.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>A sequence lists values; a series accumulates them. In general \\(u_5\\ne S_5\\), and the contextual units may differ.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Translate each symbol into words before substituting.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>For \\(2,5,8,11,\\ldots\\), compare \\(u_4\\) and \\(S_4\\).</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(u_4=11\\), while \\(S_4=26\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Constant first difference","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>An arithmetic sequence has the same difference between every pair of consecutive terms. The common difference may be positive, negative or zero. Checking all visible differences is stronger evidence than judging whether a list “looks linear.”</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[d=u_{n+1}-u_n\\qquad\\text{for every relevant }n\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>The sign of \\(d\\) describes direction; constancy of \\(d\\) defines the structure.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Constant first difference\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Constant first difference</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"97.5\" y=\"131.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">term</text><rect x=\"140.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"182.5\" y=\"131.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">14</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"267.5\" y=\"131.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">9</text><rect x=\"310.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"352.5\" y=\"131.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">4</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"437.5\" y=\"131.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">−1</text><rect x=\"480.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"522.5\" y=\"131.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">−6</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"97.5\" y=\"207.25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">difference</text><rect x=\"140.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"182.5\" y=\"207.25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">−5</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"267.5\" y=\"207.25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">−5</text><rect x=\"310.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"352.5\" y=\"207.25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">−5</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"437.5\" y=\"207.25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">−5</text><rect x=\"480.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"85.0\" height=\"75.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"522.5\" y=\"207.25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">—</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">equal first differences are the invariant</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 2 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Worked example · prove the structure","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Show that \\(14,9,4,-1,-6\\) is arithmetic and state \\(d\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Compute every visible difference</span><p>\\(9-14=-5,\\ 4-9=-5,\\ -1-4=-5,\\ -6-(-1)=-5\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Compare</span><p>Every first difference is equal.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Conclude</span><p>The sequence is arithmetic with \\(d=-5\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">A decreasing sequence can be perfectly arithmetic.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Decide whether \\(3.2,4.1,5.0,5.9,6.8\\) is arithmetic. Give evidence.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-2\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Each first difference is \\(0.9\\), so the sequence is arithmetic with \\(d=0.9\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Subtract consecutive terms in the same order. The results are \\(0.9,0.9,0.9,0.9\\). The repeated value is the common difference.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why is a table of first differences more convincing than a visual impression?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“An arithmetic sequence must increase.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>A negative common difference gives a decreasing sequence, and \\(d=0\\) gives a constant sequence.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Test consecutive differences rather than direction.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Is \\(12,12,12,12,\\ldots\\) arithmetic?</p><p><b>Check:</b> Yes; \\(d=0\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"The explicit rule and the off-by-one idea","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Starting at \\(u_1\\), reaching \\(u_n\\) requires \\(n-1\\) equal jumps. This structural count explains the explicit formula and provides a quick way to detect an off-by-one error.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[u_n=u_1+(n-1)d\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>The coefficient \\(n-1\\) counts transitions from term 1 to term \\(n\\).</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The explicit rule and the off-by-one idea\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">The explicit rule and the off-by-one idea</text>\n    <defs><marker id=\"arrow\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L7,3 z\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/></marker></defs>\n    <text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">u₁</text>\n    <text x=\"540\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">u₆</text>\n    <path d=\"M93.0 175 H159.0\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\"/><path d=\"M185.0 175 H251.0\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\"/><path d=\"M277.0 175 H343.0\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\"/><path d=\"M369.0 175 H435.0\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\"/><path d=\"M461.0 175 H527.0\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\"/><circle cx=\"80.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/><circle cx=\"172.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><circle cx=\"264.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><circle cx=\"356.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><circle cx=\"448.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">add d repeated 5 times</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">six terms are connected by five jumps</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 3 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Worked example · count jumps before substituting","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>An arithmetic sequence has \\(u_1=28\\) and \\(d=3\\). Find \\(u_{24}\\) and interpret the factor 23.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Choose the explicit form</span><p>\\(u_{24}=28+(24-1)(3)\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Count transitions</span><p>There are 23 jumps from term 1 to term 24.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Calculate</span><p>\\(u_{24}=28+69=97\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The index is not itself the number of additions.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>For \\(u_1=7.5\\) and \\(d=-0.4\\), find \\(u_{18}\\).</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-3\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(u_{18}=7.5+17(-0.4)=0.7\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>There are \\(17\\) transitions. Multiplying \\(-0.4\\) by \\(17\\) gives \\(-6.8\\), then adding the first term gives \\(0.7\\).</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Substitute \\(n=1\\) into the explicit formula. What structural check does this provide?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Use \\(u_n=u_1+nd\\).”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>This applies one extra jump and would make \\(u_1=u_1+d\\).</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Test a proposed formula at \\(n=1\\) and count transitions explicitly.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Which formula generates \\(5,8,11,\\ldots\\)?</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(u_n=5+3(n-1)=3n+2\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Recovering parameters and solving inverse problems","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Two known terms create two linear equations in \\(u_1\\) and \\(d\\). Subtracting them isolates the repeated change. When solving for an index, the result must be a positive integer because sequence inputs are discrete.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[u_p=u_1+(p-1)d,\\qquad u_q=u_1+(q-1)d\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Use the index gap \\(q-p\\) as the number of equal differences between the known terms.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Recovering parameters and solving inverse problems\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Recovering parameters and solving inverse problems</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">known term</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">equation</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">information</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">u₁₀=42</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">u₁+9d=42</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">9 jumps</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">u₁₉=87</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">u₁+18d=87</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">18 jumps</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">subtract equations to isolate repeated change</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 4 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Worked example · two terms determine the model","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Given \\(u_{10}=42\\) and \\(u_{19}=87\\), find \\(u_1\\), \\(d\\), and \\(u_{100}\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Form equations</span><p>\\(u_1+9d=42\\) and \\(u_1+18d=87\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Subtract</span><p>\\(9d=45\\Rightarrow d=5\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Back-substitute</span><p>\\(u_1=42-45=-3\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Predict</span><p>\\(u_{100}=-3+99(5)=492\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Verify the recovered rule against both supplied terms.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>An arithmetic sequence has \\(u_5=30\\) and \\(u_{13}=70\\). Find \\(u_1\\) and \\(d\\).</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-4\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(8d=40\\Rightarrow d=5\\). Then \\(u_1+4(5)=30\\Rightarrow u_1=10\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The index gap is \\(13-5=8\\), so eight equal differences account for the increase \\(70-30=40\\). Therefore \\(d=5\\), and back-substitution gives \\(u_1=10\\).</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why is dividing the change in term value by the change in index legitimate here?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“If solving for \\(n\\) gives 12.4, then the answer is term 12.4.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>A sequence is indexed by positive integers; 12.4 is not a term number.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Use an inequality or inspect consecutive integer terms, depending on the question.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>If \\(u_n=5+3(n-1)\\), is 50 a term?</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(5+3(n-1)=50\\Rightarrow n=16\\), so yes. A non-integer result would mean no exact term.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Finite arithmetic sums and model decisions","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Pairing the first and last terms shows that every pair has the same total. This gives two equivalent formulas. In applications, \\(u_n\\) answers “what happens at stage \\(n\\)?” while \\(S_n\\) answers “what has accumulated through stage \\(n\\)?”</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[S_n=\\frac n2(u_1+u_n)=\\frac n2\\bigl[2u_1+(n-1)d\\bigr]\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Choose the form that matches the information already known, then interpret the accumulated units.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Finite arithmetic sums and model decisions\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Finite arithmetic sums and model decisions</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">forward</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">3+5+7+⋯+203</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">first-to-last</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">reverse</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">203+201+⋯+3</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">last-to-first</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">each vertical pair has the same sum</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 5 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Worked example · accumulation with a large index","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>For \\(3,5,7,\\ldots\\), find \\(S_{101}\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Identify parameters</span><p>\\(u_1=3,d=2,n=101\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Find the last term</span><p>\\(u_{101}=3+100(2)=203\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Use paired-sum formula</span><p>\\(S_{101}=\\frac{101}{2}(3+203)=10403\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The sum counts 101 terms, not 100 differences.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Find the least \\(n\\) for which the sum of \\(8,13,18,\\ldots\\) exceeds 2000. Verify consecutive sums.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-5\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(S_n=\\frac n2[16+5(n-1)]=\\frac{n(5n+11)}2\\). Solving gives a threshold between 27 and 28; \\(S_{27}=1971\\) and \\(S_{28}=2114\\), so the least \\(n\\) is 28.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Model the accumulated total, solve the quadratic inequality, then enforce the integer condition. The consecutive-sum check proves minimality rather than merely producing a rounded root.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why does a threshold problem require checking both \\(S_{n-1}\\) and \\(S_n\\)?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“The total after \\(n\\) stages is \\(nu_n\\).”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>That assumes every term equals the last term, which overestimates an increasing sequence and underestimates a decreasing one.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Use the average of first and last terms for an arithmetic sequence.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Compare \\(5(13)\\) with the true sum \\(1+4+7+10+13\\).</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(65\\) versus \\(35\\); the last-term shortcut is invalid.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Interactive investigation","html":"<div class=\"u1-lab-intro\"><div><h3>Predict first</h3><p>Predict how changing the first term, common difference and number of terms affects both the last term and the cumulative sum. Test a negative difference as well as a positive one.</p><p class=\"u1-tech-note\">Use sequence or spreadsheet mode to generate terms and cumulative sums. Before trusting the output, verify the first term, one difference, the indexing convention and the units.</p></div><div class=\"lab\" id=\"lesson-lab\" data-lab=\"1.2\"></div></div>","kind":"lab","eyebrow":"Technology must expose structure"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Transparent GDC protocol","html":"<div class=\"u1-protocol\"><article><b>1</b><h3>Represent</h3><p>Enter the explicit rule or create a column of indices and a formula column.</p></article><article><b>2</b><h3>Generate</h3><p>Produce enough terms to expose the constant first difference and any threshold.</p></article><article><b>3</b><h3>Accumulate</h3><p>Create a running-total column or use a finite-sum command.</p></article><article><b>4</b><h3>Verify</h3><p>Check \\(u_1\\), one difference and one hand-calculated term or sum.</p></article><article><b>5</b><h3>Interpret</h3><p>State what the chosen term or sum means, with units and a model limitation.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-callout\"><b>IB communication:</b> record the model, relevant settings or entries, output, units and interpretation.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Use technology, but show mathematics"},{"section":"Synthesis","title":"Mixed reasoning challenge","html":"<div class=\"u1-synthesis\"><div class=\"u1-synthesis-grid\"><article><b>A</b><p>A seating plan has \\(u_5=30\\) and \\(u_{13}=70\\). Determine \\(u_1\\) and \\(d\\).</p></article><article><b>B</b><p>Write explicit formulas for \\(u_n\\) and \\(S_n\\).</p></article><article><b>C</b><p>Find the largest whole number of rows whose total does not exceed 1500 seats.</p></article><article><b>D</b><p>Explain one design feature that could invalidate the arithmetic model.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"synthesis\"></textarea><details><summary>Solution architecture</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Subtracting \\(u_{13}-u_5\\) gives \\(8d=40\\), so \\(d=5\\). Then \\(u_1+4d=30\\Rightarrow u_1=10\\). Thus \\(u_n=10+5(n-1)=5n+5\\) and \\(S_n=\\frac n2(10+5n+5)=\\frac{5n(n+3)}2\\). Solve \\(\\frac{5n(n+3)}2\\le1500\\) and verify consecutive integers. Aisles, accessibility gaps or sight-line constraints can break the constant increase.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Connect representations and constraints"},{"section":"IB-style task","title":"Extended-response preview","html":"<div class=\"u1-task\"><div class=\"u1-task-meta\"><span>Paper 2 · modelling</span><span>12 marks</span><span>GDC expected</span></div><h3>ECHS auditorium design</h3><p>An auditorium has 18 seats in row 1 and three more seats in every successive row.</p><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(a)</b><p>Write a formula for the number of seats in row \\(n\\).</p><span>[2]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(b)</b><p>Find the number of seats in row 32 and the total in the first 32 rows.</p><span>[5]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(c)</b><p>A safety limit allows at most 2500 seats. Find the greatest possible whole number of rows.</p><span>[4]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(d)</b><p>State one reason the pattern may need modification.</p><span>[1]</span></div><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"exam\">Open all 3 IB tasks</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Read the command term and the context"},{"section":"Assessment literacy","title":"How marks are earned","html":"<div class=\"u1-command-grid\"><article><b>Write down</b><p>A direct result with minimal working.</p></article><article><b>Find</b><p>A correct method and result.</p></article><article><b>Show that</b><p>A connected argument reaching the stated conclusion.</p></article><article><b>Hence</b><p>Use a result already established.</p></article><article><b>Interpret</b><p>Translate the mathematical result into the context and units.</p></article><article><b>Evaluate</b><p>Judge usefulness using evidence and limitations.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-mark-chain\"><span>model</span><i>→</i><span>method</span><i>→</i><span>accuracy</span><i>→</i><span>interpretation</span><i>→</i><span>evaluation</span></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Write for mathematical evidence"},{"section":"Exit ticket","title":"Three pieces of evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-exit-wrap\"><div class=\"u1-exit\"><article><b>1</b><p>For \\(u_1=-4,d=6\\), find \\(u_{20}\\).</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>For the same sequence, find \\(S_{20}\\).</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Explain why \\(u_n=u_1+nd\\) cannot be the correct general formula.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"exit-ticket\"></textarea><details><summary>Teacher check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Expected: \\(u_{20}=110\\), \\(S_{20}=1060\\), and the formula with \\(nd\\) fails at \\(n=1\\) because it applies one extra jump.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Independent check"},{"section":"Reflection","title":"Where this idea travels next","html":"<div class=\"u1-connections\"><article><b>Functions</b><p>An arithmetic sequence is a linear function restricted to positive integer inputs.</p></article><article><b>Finance</b><p>Regular deposits or payments can generate arithmetic or geometric structures depending on how change occurs.</p></article><article><b>Statistics</b><p>First differences help diagnose whether a linear model is plausible.</p></article><article><b>Calculus</b><p>Discrete accumulation anticipates later ideas of total change and area.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-reflection\"><h3>Return to the inquiry</h3><p>Revise your original response: what evidence proves a pattern is arithmetic, and what evidence would make you reject it as a long-term model?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"reflection\"></textarea></div><div class=\"u1-route-launch\"><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"practice\">Practice Studio</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"quiz\">Timed Quiz</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"review\">Mastery evidence</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Learn → practise → demonstrate → review"}]},"1.3":{"number":"1.3","title":"Geometric Sequences and Series","subtitle":"Model constant multiplicative change, recover ratios from data, and use finite sums and thresholds with disciplined indexing.","objectives":["Recognize geometric structure, including fractional and negative common ratios.","Use recursive and explicit forms with correct \\(n-1\\) indexing.","Recover \\(u_1\\) and \\(r\\) from separated terms, including multiple possible ratios.","Calculate and justify finite geometric sums.","Solve threshold problems and evaluate saturation or changing-rate limitations."],"vocab":["geometric sequence","common ratio","growth factor","decay factor","alternating terms","finite geometric series","threshold","extrapolation"],"technology":"Use sequence or table mode to compare term values and partial sums. For a threshold, use a table or solve logarithmically, then verify consecutive integer indices.","source_basis":["Nikolaidis Topic 1A — geometric sequences and finite sums","Supplied MAI 1.6 geometric-sequence exercise set","Pearson AI SL — multiplicative models and technology use"],"syllabus_focus":"Unit 1 — geometric sequences and finite series, growth and decay, thresholds and applications.","slides":[{"section":"Launch","title":"1.3 · Geometric Sequences and Series","html":"<div class=\"u1-cover\"><div><div class=\"u1-kicker\">IB DP Mathematics · Applications and Interpretation SL</div><h1><span>1.3</span>Geometric Sequences and Series</h1><p class=\"u1-cover-lead\">Model constant multiplicative change, recover ratios from data, and use finite sums and thresholds with disciplined indexing.</p><div class=\"u1-pill-row\"><span>36 purposeful screens</span><span>52 Practice Studio questions</span><span>14-question quiz</span><span>3 IB tasks</span></div><div class=\"u1-central-idea\"><b>Central idea</b><p>Geometric structure is governed by a constant ratio. The ratio controls growth, decay, sign and the difference between a term prediction and cumulative total.</p></div><button class=\"route-jump u1-start\" data-go=\"learn\" data-cover-next=\"1\">Begin lesson →</button></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"One ratio, many behaviors\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">One ratio, many behaviors</text>\n    <defs><marker id=\"rarr\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L7,3 z\" fill=\"#177e89\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"38.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">160</text><path d=\"M127.0 159 H186.3\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#rarr)\"/><text x=\"156.7\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">× 1.5</text><rect x=\"191.3\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"233.3\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">240</text><path d=\"M280.3 159 H339.7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#rarr)\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">× 1.5</text><rect x=\"344.7\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"386.7\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">360</text><path d=\"M433.7 159 H493.0\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#rarr)\"/><text x=\"463.3\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">× 1.5</text><rect x=\"498.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">540</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">multiplication rather than constant addition</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"cover","eyebrow":"Unit 1 · Number and Algebra"},{"section":"Launch","title":"A question worth investigating","html":"<div class=\"u1-launch\"><div><blockquote>How can the same constant-ratio idea describe rapid growth, decay toward zero, alternating signs and financial accumulation?</blockquote><p>Annual sales increase from 160 to 240 to 360. A constant ratio initially fits perfectly, but a finite population means the model cannot continue unchanged forever.</p><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"launch-inquiry\" aria-label=\"Initial inquiry response\"></textarea></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Growth eventually meets a limit\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Growth eventually meets a limit</text>\n    <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"255\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"295\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"295\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"425\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"425\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"490\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"490\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"555\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><path d=\"M120 235 C205 231 270 217 330 190 C405 155 458 113 540 78\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M120 230 L540 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 8\"/><text x=\"465\" y=\"91\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">multiplicative</text><text x=\"420\" y=\"143\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#177e89\">additive</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">models are useful within a justified domain</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"inquiry","eyebrow":"Think · discuss · revisit"},{"section":"Learning route","title":"Learning intentions and evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-goals\"><div class=\"u1-goal-grid\"><article><b>1</b><p>Recognize geometric structure, including fractional and negative common ratios.</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Use recursive and explicit forms with correct \\(n-1\\) indexing.</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Recover \\(u_1\\) and \\(r\\) from separated terms, including multiple possible ratios.</p></article><article><b>4</b><p>Calculate and justify finite geometric sums.</p></article><article><b>5</b><p>Solve threshold problems and evaluate saturation or changing-rate limitations.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-evidence\"><h3>Evidence of understanding</h3><ul><li>Verify all visible consecutive ratios and discuss zero-term restrictions.</li><li>Interpret the growth or decay factor and its associated percentage change.</li><li>Distinguish \\(u_n\\) from \\(S_n\\) and select the correct formula.</li><li>Check the first integer index satisfying a threshold and critique long-range extrapolation.</li></ul></div></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Know what success looks like"},{"section":"Diagnostic","title":"Try before instruction","html":"<div class=\"u1-diagnostic\"><div><span>Diagnostic</span><p>A culture has values \\(160,240,360,540,\\ldots\\). Identify the invariant, write the sixth term, and explain why adding 80 each time is not the model.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"diagnostic\"></textarea><details><summary>Diagnostic lens</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>A strong response identifies \\(r=1.5\\), obtains \\(u_6=1215\\), and explains that the absolute increases themselves grow.</p></div></details></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Additive or multiplicative?\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Additive or multiplicative?</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">difference</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">80, 120, 180</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">not constant</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">ratio</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">1.5, 1.5, 1.5</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">constant</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">test both structures before choosing a model</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Use errors as information"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Constant ratio, sign and magnitude","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>A geometric sequence multiplies by one constant ratio. A negative ratio alternates signs; \\(|r|<1\\) makes term magnitudes approach zero; \\(|r|>1\\) makes magnitudes grow. Ratios involving a zero term require special care because division by zero is undefined.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[r=\\frac{u_{n+1}}{u_n}\\qquad(u_n\\ne0)\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Describe \\(r\\) as a factor. A factor of 1.12 means a 12% increase; a factor of 0.83 means a 17% decrease.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Constant ratio, sign and magnitude\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Constant ratio, sign and magnitude</text>\n    <defs><marker id=\"rarr\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L7,3 z\" fill=\"#177e89\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"38.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">1</text><path d=\"M127.0 159 H148.0\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#rarr)\"/><text x=\"137.5\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">× −2</text><rect x=\"153.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"195.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">−2</text><path d=\"M242.0 159 H263.0\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#rarr)\"/><text x=\"252.5\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">× −2</text><rect x=\"268.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">4</text><path d=\"M357.0 159 H378.0\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#rarr)\"/><text x=\"367.5\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">× −2</text><rect x=\"383.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"425.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">−8</text><path d=\"M472.0 159 H493.0\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#rarr)\"/><text x=\"482.5\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">× −2</text><rect x=\"498.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">16</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">negative ratio alternates signs</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 1 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Worked example · prove a constant ratio","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Use all four terms \\(18,54,162,486\\) to show the sequence is geometric.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Calculate ratios</span><p>\\(54/18=3\\), \\(162/54=3\\), \\(486/162=3\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Compare</span><p>Every consecutive ratio is equal.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Conclude</span><p>The sequence is geometric with \\(r=3\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">One matching pair is suggestive; all visible pairs provide evidence.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Determine whether \\(64,-32,16,-8,4\\) is geometric and describe its behavior.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-1\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Every ratio is \\(-\\frac12\\). Signs alternate and magnitudes halve, so the terms approach zero.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The ratios \\(-32/64,16/(-32),-8/16,4/(-8)\\) are all \\(-1/2\\). The sign of \\(r\\) explains alternation; \\(|r|=1/2<1\\) explains decreasing magnitude.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why do the sign and absolute value of the ratio answer different questions?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“A geometric ratio must be positive and greater than 1.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>Ratios may be negative, zero in special sequences, or fractional. These cases produce alternation, immediate zeros or decay.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Analyze sign and magnitude separately.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Describe \\(5,2.5,1.25,0.625,\\ldots\\).</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(r=0.5\\); all terms stay positive and decay toward zero.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Explicit rule and index transitions","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>From \\(u_1\\) to \\(u_n\\) there are \\(n-1\\) multiplications by \\(r\\). The exponent counts transitions, just as \\(n-1\\) counted additive jumps in an arithmetic sequence.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[u_n=u_1r^{n-1}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>A geometric term rule is an exponential function restricted to positive integer inputs.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Explicit rule and index transitions\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Explicit rule and index transitions</text>\n    <defs><marker id=\"rarr\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L7,3 z\" fill=\"#177e89\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"38.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">u₁</text><path d=\"M127.0 159 H148.0\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#rarr)\"/><text x=\"137.5\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">× r</text><rect x=\"153.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"195.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">u₁r</text><path d=\"M242.0 159 H263.0\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#rarr)\"/><text x=\"252.5\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">× r</text><rect x=\"268.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">u₁r²</text><path d=\"M357.0 159 H378.0\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#rarr)\"/><text x=\"367.5\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">× r</text><rect x=\"383.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"425.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">u₁r³</text><path d=\"M472.0 159 H493.0\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#rarr)\"/><text x=\"482.5\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">× r</text><rect x=\"498.0\" y=\"125\" width=\"84\" height=\"68\" rx=\"17\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"540.0\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#17324d\">u₁r⁴</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">term 5 contains four multiplications</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 2 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Worked example · predict a distant term","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>For \\(10,20,40,80,\\ldots\\), find \\(u_{10}\\) and write \\(u_n\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Identify parameters</span><p>\\(u_1=10,r=2\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Write the rule</span><p>\\(u_n=10(2)^{n-1}\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Evaluate</span><p>\\(u_{10}=10(2)^9=5120\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Using \\(r^n\\) would shift every term by one stage.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>A sequence has \\(u_1=500\\) and \\(r=0.5\\). Find \\(u_4\\) and \\(u_{11}\\).</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-2\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(u_4=500(0.5)^3=62.5\\), and \\(u_{11}=500(0.5)^{10}=0.48828125\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Term 4 requires three multiplications; term 11 requires ten. The exponent is always one less than the term number when the first term is \\(u_1\\).</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">How does substituting \\(n=1\\) verify the indexing of the rule?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“The nth term is \\(u_1r^n\\).”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>This gives \\(u_1r\\) when \\(n=1\\), so the supposed first term is wrong.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Count transitions or test the rule at \\(n=1\\).</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Write the rule for \\(3,6,12,24,\\ldots\\).</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(u_n=3(2)^{n-1}\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Recovering a ratio from separated terms","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Dividing separated terms cancels \\(u_1\\) and produces a power of \\(r\\). If the index gap is even, a positive quotient may allow two real ratios, \\(r\\) and \\(-r\\). Context may remove one possibility.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[\\frac{u_q}{u_p}=r^{q-p}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>The exponent on \\(r\\) is the index gap, not either term number.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Recovering a ratio from separated terms\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Recovering a ratio from separated terms</text>\n    <defs><marker id=\"arrow\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L7,3 z\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/></marker></defs>\n    <text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">u₅</text>\n    <text x=\"540\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">u₇</text>\n    <path d=\"M93.0 175 H297.0\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\"/><path d=\"M323.0 175 H527.0\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\"/><circle cx=\"80.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/><circle cx=\"310.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><circle cx=\"540.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">multiply by r repeated 2 times</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">two transitions produce r²</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 3 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Worked example · two sequences can fit the same data","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>A geometric sequence has \\(u_5=3\\) and \\(u_7=0.75\\). Find every possible real \\(r\\), \\(u_1\\), and \\(u_{10}\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Divide separated terms</span><p>\\(u_7/u_5=r^2=0.75/3=0.25\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Solve for the ratio</span><p>\\(r=\\pm0.5\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Recover the first term</span><p>\\(3=u_1r^4=u_1(0.0625)\\Rightarrow u_1=48\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Find term 10</span><p>\\(u_{10}=48r^9=\\pm0.09375\\), with sign matching \\(r^9\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Positive separated terms do not force a positive ratio when the gap is even.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>A positive geometric sequence has \\(u_2=12\\) and \\(u_5=324\\). Find \\(r\\), \\(u_1\\), and \\(u_{10}\\).</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-3\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(r^3=324/12=27\\Rightarrow r=3\\). Then \\(u_1=4\\) and \\(u_{10}=4(3)^9=78732\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The index gap is three. Because the problem states that all terms are positive, the positive cube root is consistent. Back-substitution verifies both supplied terms.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">What extra contextual statement would rule out a negative ratio in the worked example?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Take \\(r=u_7/u_5\\).”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>That quotient covers two transitions, so it equals \\(r^2\\), not \\(r\\).</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Write \\(u_q/u_p=r^{q-p}\\) before calculating.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>If \\(u_3=20\\) and \\(u_6=160\\), what power of \\(r\\) equals 8?</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(r^{6-3}=r^3=8\\), so \\(r=2\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Finite geometric sums","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Multiplying a finite sum by \\(r\\) shifts every term one position. Subtracting the two aligned expressions cancels the middle terms and leaves a formula valid for \\(r\\ne1\\). If \\(r=1\\), every term is \\(u_1\\) and the sum is \\(nu_1\\).</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[S_n=u_1\\frac{1-r^n}{1-r}=u_1\\frac{r^n-1}{r-1},\\qquad r\\ne1\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Count the number of terms before using the formula; an exponent in the last term is not automatically the number of terms.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Finite geometric sums\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Finite geometric sums</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"118.75\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">expression</text><rect x=\"182.5\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"246.25\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">first</text><rect x=\"310.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"373.75\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">middle terms</text><rect x=\"437.5\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"501.25\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">last</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"118.75\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">Sₙ</text><rect x=\"182.5\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"246.25\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">u₁</text><rect x=\"310.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"373.75\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">+u₁r+⋯</text><rect x=\"437.5\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"501.25\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">+u₁rⁿ⁻¹</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"118.75\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">rSₙ</text><rect x=\"182.5\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"246.25\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">—</text><rect x=\"310.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"373.75\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">u₁r+⋯</text><rect x=\"437.5\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"501.25\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">+u₁rⁿ</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">subtract to cancel the aligned middle terms</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 4 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Worked example · count terms correctly","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Find \\(2+4+8+\\cdots+2^{10}\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Identify the first and last terms</span><p>\\(u_1=2\\), and \\(2^{10}\\) is the tenth term because \\(u_n=2^n\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Set parameters</span><p>\\(r=2,n=10\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Sum</span><p>\\(S_{10}=2\\frac{2^{10}-1}{2-1}=2046\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The exponent 10 is also the term count here only because the first term is \\(2^1\\).</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Find the sum \\(1+\\frac12+\\frac14+\\cdots+\\frac1{2^{10}}\\).</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-4\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>There are 11 terms, from exponent 0 through 10. Thus \\(S_{11}=\\frac{1-(1/2)^{11}}{1-1/2}=\\frac{2047}{1024}\\approx1.99902\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The last term is \\((1/2)^{10}\\), but the first exponent is zero. Counting inclusively gives 11 terms. This is a finite sum; no infinite-sum formula is needed.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why is counting exponents from 0 to 10 an inclusive count of 11 terms?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“The last exponent is 10, so there are 10 terms.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>The first exponent may be zero or another value. Term count depends on both endpoints.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>List the first two and last two terms, then count indices inclusively.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>How many terms are in \\(3^4+3^5+\\cdots+3^{12}\\)?</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(12-4+1=9\\) terms.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Growth, decay, thresholds and model limits","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Repeated percentage change creates a geometric model with factor \\(1+p\\) for growth or \\(1-p\\) for decay. Threshold questions require the first integer index that satisfies an inequality. A correct calculation is still only as credible as the constant-ratio assumption.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[u_n=u_1(1\\pm p)^{n-1}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>A factor is multiplicative; a percentage rate is the factor minus one, expressed as a percentage.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Growth, decay, thresholds and model limits\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Growth, decay, thresholds and model limits</text>\n    <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"255\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"295\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"295\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"425\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"425\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"490\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"490\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"555\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><path d=\"M120 235 C205 231 270 217 330 190 C405 155 458 113 540 78\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M120 230 L540 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 8\"/><text x=\"465\" y=\"91\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">multiplicative</text><text x=\"420\" y=\"143\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#177e89\">additive</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">constant additive and multiplicative models diverge over time</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 5 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Worked example · first term beyond a threshold","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>A geometric sequence has \\(u_1=5\\) and \\(u_4=40\\). Find the first term exceeding 1000.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Recover the ratio</span><p>\\(40=5r^3\\Rightarrow r^3=8\\Rightarrow r=2\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Solve the inequality</span><p>\\(5(2)^{n-1}>1000\\Rightarrow2^{n-1}>200\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Locate the integer threshold</span><p>\\(n-1>\\log_2 200\\approx7.64\\), so \\(n=9\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Verify</span><p>\\(u_8=640\\le1000\\) and \\(u_9=1280>1000\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Neighboring-term verification proves that the index is the first one.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>A value starts at 500 and decays by 12% each year. Find the first year-end value below 200.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-5\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(u_n=500(0.88)^{n-1}\\). Solving gives \\(n-1>\\ln(0.4)/\\ln(0.88)\\approx7.17\\), so \\(n=9\\). Verify \\(u_8\\approx204.34\\) and \\(u_9\\approx179.82\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The decay factor is \\(0.88\\). Because the index starts at 1, the elapsed number of annual changes is \\(n-1\\). The neighboring values establish minimality.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why can a mathematically correct geometric forecast still be a poor decision model?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“If the first three ratios match, the model is valid forever.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>Matching data support the rule only over the observed range. Capacity, competition, policy or changing conditions can alter the ratio.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>State the model domain and evaluate extrapolation against contextual constraints.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>A market has 80 000 people. Can annual phone sales grow by factor 1.5 indefinitely?</p><p><b>Check:</b> No. The finite market and replacement behavior eventually force the pattern to change.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Interactive investigation","html":"<div class=\"u1-lab-intro\"><div><h3>Predict first</h3><p>Change the ratio through values greater than 1, between 0 and 1, and negative. Predict term signs, magnitude and cumulative behavior before calculating.</p><p class=\"u1-tech-note\">Use sequence or table mode to compare term values and partial sums. For a threshold, use a table or solve logarithmically, then verify consecutive integer indices.</p></div><div class=\"lab\" id=\"lesson-lab\" data-lab=\"1.3\"></div></div>","kind":"lab","eyebrow":"Technology must expose structure"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Transparent GDC protocol","html":"<div class=\"u1-protocol\"><article><b>1</b><h3>Represent</h3><p>Enter \\(u_n=u_1r^{n-1}\\) or define a recursive column.</p></article><article><b>2</b><h3>Inspect</h3><p>Check consecutive ratios, sign behavior and the index convention.</p></article><article><b>3</b><h3>Accumulate</h3><p>Generate finite partial sums only for the required number of terms.</p></article><article><b>4</b><h3>Threshold</h3><p>Use a table or logarithmic equation, then test neighboring integer indices.</p></article><article><b>5</b><h3>Evaluate</h3><p>Compare predictions with context limits such as capacity, market size or changing rates.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-callout\"><b>IB communication:</b> record the model, relevant settings or entries, output, units and interpretation.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Use technology, but show mathematics"},{"section":"Synthesis","title":"Mixed reasoning challenge","html":"<div class=\"u1-synthesis\"><div class=\"u1-synthesis-grid\"><article><b>A</b><p>A sequence has \\(u_5=3\\) and \\(u_7=0.75\\). Find every possible real ratio.</p></article><article><b>B</b><p>Find \\(u_1\\) and \\(u_{10}\\) for each sequence.</p></article><article><b>C</b><p>Compare the first ten partial sums.</p></article><article><b>D</b><p>Explain why the sign ambiguity occurs and identify contextual evidence that could remove it.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"synthesis\"></textarea><details><summary>Solution architecture</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Dividing gives \\(r^2=0.25\\), so \\(r=\\pm0.5\\). Since \\(u_5=u_1r^4=3\\), both cases give \\(u_1=48\\). Then \\(u_{10}=48r^9\\), so the values are \\(0.09375\\) and \\(-0.09375\\). The two partial sums differ because alternating signs cause cancellation. Knowing that all terms represent positive quantities would force \\(r=0.5\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Connect representations and constraints"},{"section":"IB-style task","title":"Extended-response preview","html":"<div class=\"u1-task\"><div class=\"u1-task-meta\"><span>Paper 2 · growth model</span><span>11 marks</span><span>GDC expected</span></div><h3>Cellmania sales model</h3><p>Units sold are 160 in 1990, 240 in 1991 and 360 in 1992, forming a geometric sequence.</p><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(a)</b><p>Find the common ratio and predict sales in 2002.</p><span>[4]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(b)</b><p>Find the first year sales exceed 5000.</p><span>[3]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(c)</b><p>Find total sales from 1990 through 2002.</p><span>[2]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(d)</b><p>Use the population of 80 000 to evaluate the long-term model.</p><span>[2]</span></div><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"exam\">Open all 3 IB tasks</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Read the command term and the context"},{"section":"Assessment literacy","title":"How marks are earned","html":"<div class=\"u1-command-grid\"><article><b>Show that</b><p>Use all relevant ratios, not one pair only.</p></article><article><b>Calculate</b><p>Substitute into a stated model and report suitable accuracy.</p></article><article><b>Find</b><p>Choose and execute an efficient method.</p></article><article><b>Hence</b><p>Build directly on an earlier result.</p></article><article><b>Explain</b><p>Connect the ratio to sign, size or context.</p></article><article><b>Suggest</b><p>Offer a plausible limitation supported by the model context.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-mark-chain\"><span>model</span><i>→</i><span>method</span><i>→</i><span>accuracy</span><i>→</i><span>interpretation</span><i>→</i><span>evaluation</span></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Write for mathematical evidence"},{"section":"Exit ticket","title":"Three pieces of evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-exit-wrap\"><div class=\"u1-exit\"><article><b>1</b><p>For \\(u_1=6,r=-\\frac12\\), find \\(u_6\\).</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Find \\(S_6\\).</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Explain how \\(r\\) controls both sign and magnitude.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"exit-ticket\"></textarea><details><summary>Teacher check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Expected: \\(u_6=-\\frac{3}{16}=-0.1875\\); \\(S_6=6\\frac{1-(-1/2)^6}{1+1/2}=3.9375\\); the negative sign alternates terms while \\(|r|<1\\) shrinks magnitudes.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Independent check"},{"section":"Reflection","title":"Where this idea travels next","html":"<div class=\"u1-connections\"><article><b>Exponential models</b><p>A geometric sequence is a discrete exponential model.</p></article><article><b>Finance</b><p>Compound interest, depreciation and annuities use repeated multiplication.</p></article><article><b>Logarithms</b><p>Threshold indices can be solved by inverting exponentiation.</p></article><article><b>Statistics</b><p>Constant ratios and logarithms help inspect multiplicative change in data.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-reflection\"><h3>Return to the inquiry</h3><p>What evidence supports a constant-ratio model, and what contextual evidence would make you limit or reject its extrapolation?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"reflection\"></textarea></div><div class=\"u1-route-launch\"><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"practice\">Practice Studio</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"quiz\">Timed Quiz</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"review\">Mastery evidence</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Learn → practise → demonstrate → review"}]},"1.4":{"number":"1.4","title":"Financial Applications of Sequences","subtitle":"Translate financial language into additive or multiplicative models, compare rates fairly, and evaluate decisions after inflation, fees and depreciation.","objectives":["Distinguish simple additive interest from compound multiplicative growth.","Use compound-interest, inflation and depreciation models with consistent time units.","Convert nominal periodic rates to effective annual rates for fair comparison.","Solve inverse financial questions for time, rate or principal using technology.","Compare options using net outcomes, purchasing power and explicit assumptions."],"vocab":["principal","simple interest","compound interest","periodic rate","nominal rate","effective annual rate","depreciation","inflation","real rate"],"technology":"Use a spreadsheet or TVM/finance application only after identifying the cash-flow timing, periodic rate and period count. Recalculate one period by hand and test the result against an estimate.","source_basis":["Pearson AI SL — sequences, compound growth and modelling applications","Nikolaidis arithmetic/geometric sequence framework","Original ECHS financial contexts and decision tasks"],"syllabus_focus":"Unit 1 — financial applications of arithmetic and geometric change, compound interest, depreciation and inflation.","slides":[{"section":"Launch","title":"1.4 · Financial Applications of Sequences","html":"<div class=\"u1-cover\"><div><div class=\"u1-kicker\">IB DP Mathematics · Applications and Interpretation SL</div><h1><span>1.4</span>Financial Applications of Sequences</h1><p class=\"u1-cover-lead\">Translate financial language into additive or multiplicative models, compare rates fairly, and evaluate decisions after inflation, fees and depreciation.</p><div class=\"u1-pill-row\"><span>36 purposeful screens</span><span>52 Practice Studio questions</span><span>14-question quiz</span><span>3 IB tasks</span></div><div class=\"u1-central-idea\"><b>Central idea</b><p>Financial formulas are sequence models with time units attached. A correct decision requires matching the rate to the compounding period, preserving cash-flow meaning and evaluating assumptions.</p></div><button class=\"route-jump u1-start\" data-go=\"learn\" data-cover-next=\"1\">Begin lesson →</button></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Additive versus multiplicative money\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Additive versus multiplicative money</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">simple</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">P(1+nr)</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">fixed interest each period</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">compound</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">P(1+r)ⁿ</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">interest earns interest</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">same starting principal · different change structure</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"cover","eyebrow":"Unit 1 · Number and Algebra"},{"section":"Launch","title":"A question worth investigating","html":"<div class=\"u1-launch\"><div><blockquote>When two financial offers advertise similar percentages, what information is still missing before a rational comparison can be made?</blockquote><p>Two savings accounts advertise 5.8% annually and 5.65% nominal compounded monthly. The smaller advertised rate can produce the larger effective return, but a fixed fee can reverse the decision.</p><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"launch-inquiry\" aria-label=\"Initial inquiry response\"></textarea></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Compounding periods\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Compounding periods</text>\n    <defs><marker id=\"tarr\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L7,3 z\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><circle cx=\"70.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">0</text><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">P</text><path d=\"M84.0 155 Q130.0 90 176.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"130.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">×(1+i)</text><circle cx=\"190.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><text x=\"190.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">1</text><text x=\"190.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">P(1+i)</text><path d=\"M204.0 155 Q250.0 90 296.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"250.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">×(1+i)</text><circle cx=\"310.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">2</text><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">P(1+i)²</text><path d=\"M324.0 155 Q370.0 90 416.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"370.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">×(1+i)</text><circle cx=\"430.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><text x=\"430.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">3</text><text x=\"430.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">P(1+i)³</text><path d=\"M444.0 155 Q490.0 90 536.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"490.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">×(1+i)</text><circle cx=\"550.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"550.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">4</text><text x=\"550.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">P(1+i)⁴</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">rate and exponent must count the same period</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"inquiry","eyebrow":"Think · discuss · revisit"},{"section":"Learning route","title":"Learning intentions and evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-goals\"><div class=\"u1-goal-grid\"><article><b>1</b><p>Distinguish simple additive interest from compound multiplicative growth.</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Use compound-interest, inflation and depreciation models with consistent time units.</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Convert nominal periodic rates to effective annual rates for fair comparison.</p></article><article><b>4</b><p>Solve inverse financial questions for time, rate or principal using technology.</p></article><article><b>5</b><p>Compare options using net outcomes, purchasing power and explicit assumptions.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-evidence\"><h3>Evidence of understanding</h3><ul><li>Identify the principal, periodic rate, number of periods and final value with units.</li><li>Explain why a nominal annual rate must be divided when the exponent counts subannual periods.</li><li>Compare effective rather than nominal rates when compounding frequencies differ.</li><li>Separate nominal growth from real growth after inflation, fees or tax.</li></ul></div></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Know what success looks like"},{"section":"Diagnostic","title":"Try before instruction","html":"<div class=\"u1-diagnostic\"><div><span>Diagnostic</span><p>QAR 10 000 earns 4% for five years. Compare a simple-interest model and an annual compound-interest model, and explain why the difference grows over time.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"diagnostic\"></textarea><details><summary>Diagnostic lens</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Simple interest gives QAR 12 000; compound interest gives \\(10000(1.04)^5\\approx12166.53\\). The difference arises because compound interest earns interest on prior interest.</p></div></details></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five-year outcome\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Five-year outcome</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">simple</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">QAR 12 000</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">QAR 400 added each year</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">compound</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">QAR 12 166.53</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">4% of the changing balance</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">small annual difference accumulates</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Use errors as information"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Simple versus compound change","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Simple interest adds the same monetary amount each period, so it is arithmetic. Compound interest multiplies the current balance by the same factor, so it is geometric. The language “of the current balance” signals multiplicative change.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[A_{\\text{simple}}=P(1+nr),\\qquad A_{\\text{compound}}=P(1+r)^n\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>A rate is not a cash amount. Simple interest keeps the cash interest fixed; compound interest keeps the percentage fixed.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Simple versus compound change\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Simple versus compound change</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">simple interest</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">+Pr each period</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">arithmetic balance</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">compound interest</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">×(1+r) each period</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">geometric balance</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">identify the invariant change before choosing a formula</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 1 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Worked example · same rate, different structure","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Compare QAR 10 000 at 4% for five years under simple and annual compound interest.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Simple model</span><p>\\(A=10000(1+5\\cdot0.04)=12000\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Compound model</span><p>\\(A=10000(1.04)^5\\approx12166.53\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Compare</span><p>Compound growth is QAR 166.53 larger after five years.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Interpret</span><p>The gap grows because later interest is calculated on earlier interest.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The same advertised percentage does not imply the same change model.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>QAR 18 000 is invested at 4.6% for five years. Find the simple and annual compound balances and compare.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-1\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Simple: \\(18000(1+5\\cdot0.046)=\\text{QAR }22140\\). Compound: \\(18000(1.046)^5\\approx\\text{QAR }22538.81\\). Compound is about QAR 398.81 higher.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The simple model adds QAR \\(18000(0.046)=828\\) each year. The compound model multiplies by 1.046 each year. Report money to two decimal places only after the full calculation.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Which words in a financial context distinguish additive change from multiplicative change?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Simple and compound interest use the same formula because both use a percentage.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>The percentage is applied to different bases: the original principal versus the changing balance.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Write the recurrence for one period before using a closed formula.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>A loan fee is QAR 100 every month. Is that additive or multiplicative?</p><p><b>Check:</b> Additive; the same cash amount is added each month.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Periodic rates and synchronized time units","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>If a nominal annual rate \\(j\\) is compounded \\(m\\) times per year, the periodic rate is \\(j/m\\) and the number of periods over \\(t\\) years is \\(mt\\). The rate and exponent must describe the same period.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[A=P\\left(1+\\frac jm\\right)^{mt}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>“6% nominal compounded monthly” means 0.5% per month, not 6% per month.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Periodic rates and synchronized time units\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Periodic rates and synchronized time units</text>\n    <defs><marker id=\"tarr\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L7,3 z\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><circle cx=\"70.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">Jan</text><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">P</text><path d=\"M84.0 155 Q130.0 90 176.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"130.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">quarter 1</text><circle cx=\"190.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><text x=\"190.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">Apr</text><text x=\"190.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">P(1+j/4)</text><path d=\"M204.0 155 Q250.0 90 296.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"250.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">quarter 2</text><circle cx=\"310.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">Jul</text><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">…</text><path d=\"M324.0 155 Q370.0 90 416.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"370.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">quarter 3</text><circle cx=\"430.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><text x=\"430.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">Oct</text><text x=\"430.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">…</text><path d=\"M444.0 155 Q490.0 90 536.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"490.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">quarter 4</text><circle cx=\"550.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"550.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">year end</text><text x=\"550.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">P(1+j/4)⁴</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">four quarterly rates · four quarterly periods</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 2 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Worked example · quarterly compounding","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Find the value of QAR 12 000 at 5.4% nominal compounded quarterly for three years.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Periodic rate</span><p>\\(i=0.054/4=0.0135\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Period count</span><p>\\(n=4(3)=12\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Model</span><p>\\(A=12000(1.0135)^{12}\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Calculate</span><p>\\(A\\approx\\text{QAR }14095.04\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The quarterly rate and quarterly exponent are synchronized.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Find the value of QAR 14 000 at 7.2% nominal compounded monthly for two years.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-2\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Monthly rate \\(=0.072/12=0.006\\); periods \\(=24\\). Thus \\(A=14000(1.006)^{24}\\approx\\text{QAR }16161.42\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The annual label is converted into a monthly rate before using a monthly period count. A reasonableness check is that the two-year increase should be somewhat above 14.4%, because of compounding.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">What error occurs if the exponent counts months but the rate remains annual?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“For 6% monthly compounding over three years, use \\((1.06)^{36}\\).”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>This applies 6% every month, not 6% nominal per year.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Divide the nominal annual rate by 12 when the exponent counts months.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>What monthly factor represents 9% nominal compounded monthly?</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(1+0.09/12=1.0075\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Depreciation, inflation and real purchasing power","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Depreciation uses a factor below one; inflation uses a factor above one. To compare purchasing power, divide the nominal growth factor by the inflation factor. Subtracting percentages is a useful approximation only when rates are small.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[V_n=V_0(1-d)^n,\\qquad \\text{real factor}=\\frac{1+r}{1+i}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>A 17% depreciation rate means retaining 83% of value: factor 0.83.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Depreciation, inflation and real purchasing power\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Depreciation, inflation and real purchasing power</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">nominal value</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">×(1+r)</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">money amount</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">purchasing power</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">×(1+r)/(1+i)</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">inflation-adjusted</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">a rising balance can still lose real value</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 3 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Worked example · asset value and replacement cost","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>A vehicle worth QAR 48 000 depreciates 17% annually. Find its value after four years. If a replacement price inflates by 3%, explain why the funding gap grows from both directions.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Depreciation factor</span><p>\\(1-0.17=0.83\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Asset value</span><p>\\(48000(0.83)^4\\approx\\text{QAR }22779.99\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Replacement factor</span><p>A new vehicle price is multiplied by \\(1.03^4\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Interpret</span><p>The old asset falls while the replacement cost rises.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">A replacement plan should model both processes, not only depreciation.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>An investment earns 7% nominal while prices rise 4%. Find the exact one-year real rate.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-3\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Real factor \\(=1.07/1.04\\approx1.028846\\), so the real rate is about \\(2.8846\\%\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Subtracting gives 3%, but the exact comparison divides multiplicative factors. The result is \\(1.07/1.04-1\\approx0.028846\\).</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why can a positive nominal return still correspond to negative purchasing-power growth?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“A 20% fall followed by a 20% rise returns to the starting value.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>The percentages act on different bases: \\(0.8\\times1.2=0.96\\).</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Multiply factors rather than cancel percentage labels.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>What percentage rise reverses a 20% fall?</p><p><b>Check:</b> From 80 back to 100 requires \\(20/80=25\\%\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Nominal and effective annual rates","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>The effective annual rate is the actual one-year percentage change after all within-year compounding. It allows offers with different compounding frequencies to be compared on a common basis.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[r_{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\left(1+\\frac jm\\right)^m-1\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Nominal is an annual label; effective is the actual annual factor minus one.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Nominal and effective annual rates\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Nominal and effective annual rates</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"118.75\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">offer</text><rect x=\"182.5\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"246.25\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">nominal</text><rect x=\"310.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"373.75\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">frequency</text><rect x=\"437.5\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"501.25\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">effective</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"118.75\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">A</text><rect x=\"182.5\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"246.25\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">5.80%</text><rect x=\"310.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"373.75\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">annual</text><rect x=\"437.5\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"501.25\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">5.800%</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"118.75\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">B</text><rect x=\"182.5\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"246.25\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">5.65%</text><rect x=\"310.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"373.75\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">monthly</text><rect x=\"437.5\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"501.25\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">≈5.799%</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">compare effective annual outcomes, then include fees</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 4 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Worked example · an advertised-rate comparison","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Calculate the effective annual rate of 4.8% nominal compounded quarterly.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Quarterly rate</span><p>\\(0.048/4=0.012\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>One-year factor</span><p>\\((1.012)^4\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Effective rate</span><p>\\((1.012)^4-1\\approx0.04887\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Communicate</span><p>About \\(4.887\\%\\) effective annually.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Frequency matters even when nominal rates look similar.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>A nominal rate is 5.1% compounded monthly. Find the effective annual rate.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-4\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\((1+0.051/12)^{12}-1\\approx0.05221\\), so the effective annual rate is about \\(5.221\\%\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The monthly rate is \\(0.00425\\). Twelve monthly multiplications produce the one-year factor. Subtract one and convert to a percentage.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why is it unfair to compare a monthly-compounded nominal rate directly with an annual effective rate?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“More frequent compounding always makes an offer better.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>Frequency helps only relative to the rate and fees. A lower nominal rate or fixed charges can outweigh the compounding advantage.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Compare net balances over the same horizon.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>What additional data are needed beyond effective rate?</p><p><b>Check:</b> Fees, taxes, risk, timing, access rules and rate guarantees.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Inverse questions and decision evaluation","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Financial questions often ask for the time, rate or starting principal rather than the final value. Technology or logarithms can invert exponential growth, but the result must be interpreted in whole periods when transactions occur discretely.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[n=\\frac{\\ln(A/P)}{\\ln(1+r)},\\qquad P=\\frac{A}{(1+r)^n}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>A decimal time is a model result; the decision may require the next completed month or year.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Inverse questions and decision evaluation\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Inverse questions and decision evaluation</text>\n    <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"255\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"295\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"295\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"425\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"425\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"490\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"490\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"555\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><path d=\"M120 235 C205 231 270 217 330 190 C405 155 458 113 540 78\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M120 230 L540 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 8\"/><text x=\"465\" y=\"91\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">multiplicative</text><text x=\"420\" y=\"143\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#177e89\">additive</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">solve continuously · decide discretely · verify neighboring periods</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 5 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Worked example · first completed year beyond a target","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>How many completed years are required for QAR 7000 to exceed QAR 10 000 at 4.5% annual compounding?</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Model</span><p>\\(7000(1.045)^n>10000\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Invert</span><p>\\(n>\\ln(10000/7000)/\\ln1.045\\approx8.10\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Apply period condition</span><p>The first completed whole year is \\(n=9\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Verify</span><p>Year 8 is below the target and year 9 is above it.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Rounding a threshold down would answer a different question.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>A machine is worth QAR 50 000 after five years of constant depreciation from QAR 90 000. Find the annual depreciation rate.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-5\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\((1-d)^5=50000/90000\\), so \\(d=1-(5/9)^{1/5}\\approx0.110910\\), or about \\(11.09\\%\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Take the fifth root of the retained-value ratio to find the annual retention factor, then subtract it from one to obtain the depreciation rate.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Which financial inputs are assumptions rather than measured facts, and how should that affect a recommendation?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“The option with the highest final balance is automatically best.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>A decision may also depend on liquidity, risk, fees, timing, tax and whether the rate is guaranteed.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>State the objective and constraints before ranking options.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Why might a lower-return account be chosen?</p><p><b>Check:</b> It may have lower risk, immediate access, lower fees or a guaranteed rate.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Interactive investigation","html":"<div class=\"u1-lab-intro\"><div><h3>Predict first</h3><p>Vary principal, nominal rate, compounding frequency and years. Predict which input changes the exponent, which changes the periodic rate and which changes both.</p><p class=\"u1-tech-note\">Use a spreadsheet or TVM/finance application only after identifying the cash-flow timing, periodic rate and period count. Recalculate one period by hand and test the result against an estimate.</p></div><div class=\"lab\" id=\"lesson-lab\" data-lab=\"1.4\"></div></div>","kind":"lab","eyebrow":"Technology must expose structure"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Transparent GDC protocol","html":"<div class=\"u1-protocol\"><article><b>1</b><h3>Translate</h3><p>Write the mathematical model before using a finance menu.</p></article><article><b>2</b><h3>Synchronize</h3><p>Rate per period and number of periods must use the same time unit.</p></article><article><b>3</b><h3>Compute</h3><p>Retain guard digits and distinguish a balance from interest earned.</p></article><article><b>4</b><h3>Compare</h3><p>Convert offers to a common time horizon and effective rate.</p></article><article><b>5</b><h3>Evaluate</h3><p>Account for fees, tax, inflation, liquidity and model assumptions.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-callout\"><b>IB communication:</b> record the model, relevant settings or entries, output, units and interpretation.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Use technology, but show mathematics"},{"section":"Synthesis","title":"Mixed reasoning challenge","html":"<div class=\"u1-synthesis\"><div class=\"u1-synthesis-grid\"><article><b>A</b><p>Offer A pays 5.8% annually. Offer B pays 5.65% nominal compounded monthly.</p></article><article><b>B</b><p>Calculate each effective annual rate and the four-year balance on QAR 20 000.</p></article><article><b>C</b><p>Offer B charges QAR 40 at the end of each year. Determine the net winner.</p></article><article><b>D</b><p>State one non-numerical factor that could change the decision.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"synthesis\"></textarea><details><summary>Solution architecture</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Offer A has effective rate 5.8%. Offer B has \\((1+0.0565/12)^{12}-1\\approx0.057986\\), or about 5.799%. Before fees the balances are very close. Subtract the four annual fees from B at their actual timing or model them explicitly; the fee is large enough to make A preferable. Liquidity, risk, access conditions or rate guarantees also matter.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Connect representations and constraints"},{"section":"IB-style task","title":"Extended-response preview","html":"<div class=\"u1-task\"><div class=\"u1-task-meta\"><span>Paper 2 · financial modelling</span><span>11 marks</span><span>GDC expected</span></div><h3>Vehicle replacement plan</h3><p>A vehicle worth QAR 140 000 depreciates 17% annually. The replacement price rises by 3% annually.</p><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(a)</b><p>Find the vehicle value after five years.</p><span>[2]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(b)</b><p>Find the replacement price after five years.</p><span>[2]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(c)</b><p>Calculate the funding gap.</p><span>[2]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(d)</b><p>Find the annual deposit required under a stated savings rate.</p><span>[3]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(e)</b><p>Evaluate one assumption in the plan.</p><span>[2]</span></div><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"exam\">Open all 3 IB tasks</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Read the command term and the context"},{"section":"Assessment literacy","title":"How marks are earned","html":"<div class=\"u1-command-grid\"><article><b>Calculate</b><p>Use the appropriate financial model and report suitable currency accuracy.</p></article><article><b>Determine</b><p>Select a method and justify the chosen variables.</p></article><article><b>Compare</b><p>Place alternatives on the same basis before judging.</p></article><article><b>Interpret</b><p>Explain the meaning of a rate, balance or gap.</p></article><article><b>Evaluate</b><p>Discuss assumptions and decision consequences.</p></article><article><b>State</b><p>Give a concise relevant fact or limitation.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-mark-chain\"><span>model</span><i>→</i><span>method</span><i>→</i><span>accuracy</span><i>→</i><span>interpretation</span><i>→</i><span>evaluation</span></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Write for mathematical evidence"},{"section":"Exit ticket","title":"Three pieces of evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-exit-wrap\"><div class=\"u1-exit\"><article><b>1</b><p>Find the balance of QAR 25 000 at 4.2% annually for six years.</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Find the effective annual rate of 4.8% nominal compounded quarterly.</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Explain why “return minus inflation” is only an approximation to the real rate.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"exit-ticket\"></textarea><details><summary>Teacher check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Expected balance \\(25000(1.042)^6\\approx\\text{QAR }31999.73\\); effective rate \\((1+0.048/4)^4-1\\approx4.887\\%\\); exact real factor is \\((1+r)/(1+i)\\), not simply \\(1+r-i\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Independent check"},{"section":"Reflection","title":"Where this idea travels next","html":"<div class=\"u1-connections\"><article><b>Geometric sequences</b><p>Compound balances form a geometric sequence.</p></article><article><b>Logarithms</b><p>Inverse questions for time or rate invert exponential growth.</p></article><article><b>Statistics</b><p>Historical returns vary; a fixed rate is a model, not a guarantee.</p></article><article><b>Ethics and decisions</b><p>Fees, risk and inflation affect different groups differently.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-reflection\"><h3>Return to the inquiry</h3><p>What information would you demand before accepting a financial comparison based only on advertised percentages?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"reflection\"></textarea></div><div class=\"u1-route-launch\"><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"practice\">Practice Studio</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"quiz\">Timed Quiz</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"review\">Mastery evidence</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Learn → practise → demonstrate → review"}]},"1.5":{"number":"1.5","title":"Exponent Laws and Logarithms","subtitle":"Build exponent laws from structure, interpret logarithms as inverse exponents, and solve exponential equations with domain and model checks.","objectives":["Simplify expressions using exponent laws with zero, negative and rational exponents.","Explain restrictions for real-valued powers and logarithms.","Solve exponential equations using common bases or logarithms.","Interpret common and natural logarithms in contextual scales and models.","Verify solutions by substitution, domain checks and contextual reasonableness."],"vocab":["base","exponent","zero exponent","negative exponent","rational exponent","logarithm","common logarithm","natural logarithm","domain","change of base"],"technology":"Use logarithm and exponential keys transparently. Record the equation being solved, the change-of-base expression or graph intersection, and a substitution check in the original model.","source_basis":["Nikolaidis Topic 1A — exponent definitions, laws and simple exponential equations","Supplied MAI 1.2–1.3 exponent exercise set","Pearson AI SL — exponents and logarithms"],"syllabus_focus":"Unit 1 — exponent laws, rational exponents, logarithms and exponential equations.","slides":[{"section":"Launch","title":"1.5 · Exponent Laws and Logarithms","html":"<div class=\"u1-cover\"><div><div class=\"u1-kicker\">IB DP Mathematics · Applications and Interpretation SL</div><h1><span>1.5</span>Exponent Laws and Logarithms</h1><p class=\"u1-cover-lead\">Build exponent laws from structure, interpret logarithms as inverse exponents, and solve exponential equations with domain and model checks.</p><div class=\"u1-pill-row\"><span>36 purposeful screens</span><span>52 Practice Studio questions</span><span>14-question quiz</span><span>3 IB tasks</span></div><div class=\"u1-central-idea\"><b>Central idea</b><p>Exponent laws describe repeated multiplicative structure; logarithms answer the inverse question “which exponent?”. Both require attention to conditions, domains and interpretation.</p></div><button class=\"route-jump u1-start\" data-go=\"learn\" data-cover-next=\"1\">Begin lesson →</button></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A process and its inverse\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">A process and its inverse</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">exponential</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">bˣ = y</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">build a value from an exponent</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">logarithm</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">log_b y = x</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">recover the exponent</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">inverse relationships with domain conditions</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"cover","eyebrow":"Unit 1 · Number and Algebra"},{"section":"Launch","title":"A question worth investigating","html":"<div class=\"u1-launch\"><div><blockquote>How can one operation describe both repeated growth and the number of growth steps needed to reach a target?</blockquote><p>A medicine decays exponentially, earthquake intensity is reported logarithmically, and pH compresses enormous concentration ranges into manageable numbers. Each context uses powers and their inverse.</p><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"launch-inquiry\" aria-label=\"Initial inquiry response\"></textarea></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Exponential and logarithmic inverses\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Exponential and logarithmic inverses</text>\n    <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"255\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"295\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"295\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"425\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"425\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"490\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"490\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"555\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><path d=\"M125 235 C220 225 285 195 340 150 C405 98 470 80 545 72\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M125 238 C185 175 260 125 350 98 C430 74 490 68 545 66\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 8\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"545\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 7\"/><text x=\"465\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">y=x</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">reflection in y=x connects a function and its inverse</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"inquiry","eyebrow":"Think · discuss · revisit"},{"section":"Learning route","title":"Learning intentions and evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-goals\"><div class=\"u1-goal-grid\"><article><b>1</b><p>Simplify expressions using exponent laws with zero, negative and rational exponents.</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Explain restrictions for real-valued powers and logarithms.</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Solve exponential equations using common bases or logarithms.</p></article><article><b>4</b><p>Interpret common and natural logarithms in contextual scales and models.</p></article><article><b>5</b><p>Verify solutions by substitution, domain checks and contextual reasonableness.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-evidence\"><h3>Evidence of understanding</h3><ul><li>State the relevant exponent law and its base restrictions.</li><li>Move between exponential and logarithmic forms fluently.</li><li>Choose common-base reasoning when exact and logarithmic inversion when needed.</li><li>Reject inadmissible logarithmic solutions and evaluate constant-rate assumptions.</li></ul></div></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Know what success looks like"},{"section":"Diagnostic","title":"Try before instruction","html":"<div class=\"u1-diagnostic\"><div><span>Diagnostic</span><p>Simplify \\(2^5\\cdot2^{-3}\\), evaluate \\(27^{2/3}\\), and solve \\(4^{x+1}=2^{10}\\). Explain each rule rather than only giving answers.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"diagnostic\"></textarea><details><summary>Diagnostic lens</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Expected: \\(2^2=4\\), \\((\\sqrt[3]{27})^2=9\\), and \\(2^{2x+2}=2^{10}\\Rightarrow x=4\\). Look for structural explanations and restrictions.</p></div></details></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Choose the structural move\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Choose the structural move</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">expression</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">move</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">result</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">2⁵·2⁻³</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">add exponents</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">2²</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">27²ᐟ³</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">root then power</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">9</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">4ˣ⁺¹=2¹⁰</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">common base</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">x=4</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">name the law and its conditions</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Use errors as information"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Exponent laws and the structure of powers","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>For the same non-zero base, multiplication adds exponents, division subtracts them and a power of a power multiplies exponents. A power distributes over a product or quotient, but not over a sum.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[a^ma^n=a^{m+n},\\quad \\frac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n},\\quad(a^m)^n=a^{mn}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>State “same base” before combining exponents and keep the condition \\(a\\ne0\\) when division is involved.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Exponent laws and the structure of powers\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Exponent laws and the structure of powers</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">structure</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">operation on exponents</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">condition</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">product</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">add</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">same base</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">quotient</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">subtract</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">same nonzero base</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">power of power</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">multiply</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">defined expression</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">exponent laws encode multiplicative structure</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 1 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Worked example · combine several laws","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Simplify \\(\\frac{x^7x^{-3}}{x^2}\\), where \\(x\\ne0\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Combine the numerator</span><p>\\(x^7x^{-3}=x^{4}\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Divide same bases</span><p>\\(x^4/x^2=x^{4-2}\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Conclude</span><p>\\(x^2\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The non-zero restriction comes from the original division by \\(x^2\\).</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Simplify \\(\\frac{(a^3)^4a^{-5}}{a^2}\\), with \\(a\\ne0\\).</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-1\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\((a^3)^4=a^{12}\\), so the total exponent is \\(12-5-2=5\\). The expression simplifies to \\(a^5\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Multiplying the exponents inside the power of a power gives 12. Then multiplication by \\(a^{-5}\\) and division by \\(a^2\\) combine as exponent arithmetic.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why does \\((a+b)^2\\ne a^2+b^2\\) in general?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Powers distribute over addition: \\((a+b)^n=a^n+b^n\\).”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>Expansion creates cross terms. For \\(n=2\\), \\((a+b)^2=a^2+2ab+b^2\\).</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Use a numerical counterexample or expand the product.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Compare \\((1+1)^3\\) with \\(1^3+1^3\\).</p><p><b>Check:</b> 8 versus 2, so the claimed law is false.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Zero, negative and rational exponents","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Zero and negative exponents extend the quotient law; rational exponents connect powers and roots. Real-valued expressions require attention to the sign of the base and the denominator of the rational exponent.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[a^0=1,\\quad a^{-m}=\\frac1{a^m},\\quad a^{m/n}=\\sqrt[n]{a^m}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>A negative exponent creates a reciprocal; it does not make the value negative.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Zero, negative and rational exponents\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Zero, negative and rational exponents</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">exponent</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">structural meaning</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">example</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">0</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">multiplicative identity</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">5⁰=1</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">−m</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">reciprocal</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">4⁻²=1/16</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">m/n</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">root and power</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">27²ᐟ³=9</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">extensions preserve the exponent laws</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 2 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Worked example · evaluate a rational negative exponent","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Evaluate \\((16/81)^{-1/2}\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Use the negative exponent</span><p>\\((16/81)^{-1/2}=(81/16)^{1/2}\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Use the half exponent</span><p>\\(\\sqrt{81/16}=9/4\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>State the value</span><p>\\(9/4=2.25\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Reciprocal and root operations are both visible in the exponent.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Evaluate \\(32^{-2/5}\\) exactly.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-2\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(32^{1/5}=2\\), so \\(32^{2/5}=4\\), and \\(32^{-2/5}=1/4\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Take the fifth root first or use \\(32=2^5\\). The negative sign then takes the reciprocal.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why can the real value of a rational power depend on whether a root index is even or odd?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“\\(a^{-3}=-a^3\\).”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>The minus sign belongs to the exponent and means reciprocal: \\(a^{-3}=1/a^3\\).</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Rewrite every negative exponent using a reciprocal before evaluating.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Compare \\(2^{-3}\\) and \\(-2^3\\).</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(2^{-3}=1/8\\), while \\(-2^3=-8\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Solving with a common base","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>If both sides can be written as powers of the same positive base other than one, equality of the powers implies equality of the exponents. This gives exact solutions without logarithmic approximation.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[a^{f(x)}=a^{g(x)},\\ a>0,\\ a\\ne1\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad f(x)=g(x)\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>First justify the common base; then equate exponents.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Solving with a common base\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Solving with a common base</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">original base</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">rewrite</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">common base</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">4</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">2²</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">2</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">8</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">2³</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">2</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">27</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">3³</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">3</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">common bases expose a linear equation in the exponents</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 3 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Worked example · rewrite before equating","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Solve \\(9^{2x-1}=27^x\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Rewrite bases</span><p>\\(9=3^2\\) and \\(27=3^3\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Apply power of a power</span><p>\\(3^{4x-2}=3^{3x}\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Equate exponents</span><p>\\(4x-2=3x\\Rightarrow x=2\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Verify</span><p>Both sides equal \\(9^3=729\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Exact structure is preferable to decimal logarithms when available.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Solve \\(4^{x+1}=8^{2x-1}\\).</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-3\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(2^{2x+2}=2^{6x-3}\\), so \\(2x+2=6x-3\\Rightarrow x=5/4\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Both sides are powers of 2. Multiplying exponents correctly is essential: \\((2^2)^{x+1}=2^{2x+2}\\) and \\((2^3)^{2x-1}=2^{6x-3}\\).</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">When would forcing a common base be inefficient or impossible?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“If the bases differ, equate the exponents anyway.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>Equal exponents do not make unequal bases equal. The implication requires the same base.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Rewrite to a genuine common base or use logarithms.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Does \\(2^x=3^x\\) imply every \\(x\\) works?</p><p><b>Check:</b> No; the only real solution is \\(x=0\\), found by analysis rather than equating exponents.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Logarithms as inverse exponents","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>A logarithm names the exponent needed to produce a positive argument from a positive base other than one. Common logarithms use base 10; natural logarithms use base \\(e\\). The argument must be positive.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[y=b^x\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x=\\log_b y\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Read \\(\\log_b y\\) as “the exponent on \\(b\\) that gives \\(y\\).”</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Logarithms as inverse exponents\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Logarithms as inverse exponents</text>\n    <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"255\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"295\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"295\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"425\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"425\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"490\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"490\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"555\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><path d=\"M125 235 C220 225 285 195 340 150 C405 98 470 80 545 72\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M125 238 C185 175 260 125 350 98 C430 74 490 68 545 66\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 8\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"545\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 7\"/><text x=\"465\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">y=x</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">exponential and logarithmic graphs reflect across y=x</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 4 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Worked example · change of base","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Evaluate \\(\\log_3 20\\) to 3 d.p.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Use an available base</span><p>\\(\\log_3 20=\\ln20/\\ln3\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Calculate</span><p>\\(\\ln20/\\ln3\\approx2.7268\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Round</span><p>\\(\\log_3 20\\approx2.727\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Check scale</span><p>\\(3^2<20<3^3\\), so the answer must lie between 2 and 3.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">An interval check catches many calculator-entry errors.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Rewrite \\(10^{-4}=0.0001\\) logarithmically and evaluate \\(\\log_5(1/125)\\).</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-4\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(\\log_{10}(0.0001)=-4\\). Since \\(1/125=5^{-3}\\), \\(\\log_5(1/125)=-3\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The logarithm records the exponent exactly. Negative values are allowed as logarithm outputs; the restriction is that the argument must be positive.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why may a logarithm be negative even though its argument must be positive?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“\\(\\log(-10)=-1\\) because \\(10^{-1}=0.1\\).”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>The logarithm argument is \\(-10\\), which is not positive in the real-number system.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Check base and argument domains before applying any log rule.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Which is defined: \\(\\log_{10}(0.1)\\) or \\(\\log_{10}(-0.1)\\)?</p><p><b>Check:</b> Only \\(\\log_{10}(0.1)=-1\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Exponential equations, logarithmic scales and model checks","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>When no convenient common base exists, logarithms isolate the exponent. Contexts such as pH and earthquake magnitude are logarithmic, so equal additive changes on the scale represent equal multiplicative factors in the underlying quantity.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[a^x=b\\Rightarrow x=\\frac{\\ln b}{\\ln a},\\qquad pH=-\\log_{10}[H^+]\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>A logarithmic-scale difference is interpreted as a ratio in the original quantity.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Exponential equations, logarithmic scales and model checks\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Exponential equations, logarithmic scales and model checks</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">scale change</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">+1 pH or Richter unit</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">additive label</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">underlying quantity</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">factor 10</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">multiplicative reality</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">logarithms compress wide ranges</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 5 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Worked example · solve and interpret","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Solve \\(650(1.14)^t=3000\\) and interpret the result.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Isolate the power</span><p>\\((1.14)^t=3000/650\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Take logarithms</span><p>\\(t=\\ln(3000/650)/\\ln1.14\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Calculate</span><p>\\(t\\approx11.672\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Interpret</span><p>The model reaches 3000 after about 11.7 time units; a discrete question may require the next whole period.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The units of \\(t\\) come from the model, not the logarithm key.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>The pH changes from 6.2 to 4.7. How many times larger is the hydrogen-ion concentration?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-5\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>The concentration ratio is \\(10^{6.2-4.7}=10^{1.5}\\approx31.6\\). The lower-pH sample has about 31.6 times the concentration.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Because pH is the negative logarithm of concentration, decreasing pH by 1.5 corresponds to multiplying concentration by \\(10^{1.5}\\).</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why should an exponential solution be substituted back into the original model?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“A difference of 2 on a logarithmic scale means twice as much.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>On a base-10 logarithmic scale, a difference of 2 represents a factor of \\(10^2=100\\).</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Convert scale differences back through the exponential inverse.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>How much more intense is magnitude 7 than magnitude 5?</p><p><b>Check:</b> A factor of \\(10^{7-5}=100\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Interactive investigation","html":"<div class=\"u1-lab-intro\"><div><h3>Predict first</h3><p>Change the logarithm base and argument, then compare exact powers with calculator approximations. Investigate how target-to-initial ratios affect a solved time.</p><p class=\"u1-tech-note\">Use logarithm and exponential keys transparently. Record the equation being solved, the change-of-base expression or graph intersection, and a substitution check in the original model.</p></div><div class=\"lab\" id=\"lesson-lab\" data-lab=\"1.5\"></div></div>","kind":"lab","eyebrow":"Technology must expose structure"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Transparent GDC protocol","html":"<div class=\"u1-protocol\"><article><b>1</b><h3>Simplify first</h3><p>Use exact exponent structure before reaching for a decimal approximation.</p></article><article><b>2</b><h3>Choose an inverse</h3><p>Use common bases, logarithms or graph intersection as appropriate.</p></article><article><b>3</b><h3>Record</h3><p>Show the equation and calculator entry, not only the output.</p></article><article><b>4</b><h3>Check domain</h3><p>Logarithm bases and arguments must be admissible.</p></article><article><b>5</b><h3>Verify</h3><p>Substitute into the original equation and interpret units or scale.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-callout\"><b>IB communication:</b> record the model, relevant settings or entries, output, units and interpretation.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Use technology, but show mathematics"},{"section":"Synthesis","title":"Mixed reasoning challenge","html":"<div class=\"u1-synthesis\"><div class=\"u1-synthesis-grid\"><article><b>A</b><p>A medicine follows \\(M(t)=120e^{-0.16t}\\) mg.</p></article><article><b>B</b><p>Find \\(M(5)\\), the half-life and the first time \\(M<20\\).</p></article><article><b>C</b><p>Rewrite the model using a per-hour factor \\(b^t\\).</p></article><article><b>D</b><p>Evaluate one reason biological data may not follow one exponential rule indefinitely.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"synthesis\"></textarea><details><summary>Solution architecture</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(M(5)=120e^{-0.8}\\approx53.9\\) mg. Half-life \\(=\\ln2/0.16\\approx4.33\\) h. The threshold satisfies \\(t>\\ln(20/120)/(-0.16)\\approx11.2\\) h. Since \\(e^{-0.16t}=(e^{-0.16})^t\\), the hourly factor is about 0.8521. Biological absorption and multi-compartment behavior can change the rate.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Connect representations and constraints"},{"section":"IB-style task","title":"Extended-response preview","html":"<div class=\"u1-task\"><div class=\"u1-task-meta\"><span>Paper 2 · exponential model</span><span>10 marks</span><span>GDC expected</span></div><h3>Medicine decay model</h3><p>A dose is modelled by \\(M(t)=120e^{-0.16t}\\) mg.</p><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(a)</b><p>Calculate the amount after five hours.</p><span>[2]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(b)</b><p>Find the half-life.</p><span>[3]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(c)</b><p>Find when the amount first falls below 20 mg.</p><span>[3]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(d)</b><p>State one limitation of the model.</p><span>[2]</span></div><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"exam\">Open all 3 IB tasks</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Read the command term and the context"},{"section":"Assessment literacy","title":"How marks are earned","html":"<div class=\"u1-command-grid\"><article><b>Simplify</b><p>Use laws to produce an equivalent expression in an efficient form.</p></article><article><b>Solve</b><p>Find all admissible values and verify them.</p></article><article><b>Evaluate</b><p>Calculate a numerical value, usually with stated accuracy.</p></article><article><b>Show that</b><p>Give a connected exact argument.</p></article><article><b>Interpret</b><p>Explain the exponent, rate or logarithmic scale in context.</p></article><article><b>Comment</b><p>Use mathematical evidence to make a concise judgement.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-mark-chain\"><span>model</span><i>→</i><span>method</span><i>→</i><span>accuracy</span><i>→</i><span>interpretation</span><i>→</i><span>evaluation</span></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Write for mathematical evidence"},{"section":"Exit ticket","title":"Three pieces of evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-exit-wrap\"><div class=\"u1-exit\"><article><b>1</b><p>Evaluate \\(81^{-3/4}\\).</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Solve \\(5^x=42\\) to 3 d.p.</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Explain why \\(\\log(x-2)\\) requires \\(x>2\\).</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"exit-ticket\"></textarea><details><summary>Teacher check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Expected \\(81^{-3/4}=1/27\\); \\(x=\\ln42/\\ln5\\approx2.322\\); the logarithm argument must be positive.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Independent check"},{"section":"Reflection","title":"Where this idea travels next","html":"<div class=\"u1-connections\"><article><b>Financial models</b><p>Logarithms solve for time or rate in compound growth.</p></article><article><b>Science</b><p>Half-life, pH and intensity scales use exponentials and logs.</p></article><article><b>Functions</b><p>Exponential and logarithmic functions are inverses.</p></article><article><b>Calculus</b><p>The constant \\(e\\) later simplifies rates of change and accumulation.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-reflection\"><h3>Return to the inquiry</h3><p>Which equations are best solved by common bases, which require logarithms, and what checks prevent a formally correct but inadmissible answer?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"reflection\"></textarea></div><div class=\"u1-route-launch\"><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"practice\">Practice Studio</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"quiz\">Timed Quiz</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"review\">Mastery evidence</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Learn → practise → demonstrate → review"}]},"1.6":{"number":"1.6","title":"Approximation, Bounds and Percentage Error","subtitle":"Communicate precision honestly, construct half-open bounds, propagate measurement uncertainty and judge the significance of error.","objectives":["Round appropriately to decimal places, significant figures and practical units.","Distinguish exact values, estimates, approximations and calculator displays.","Construct half-open intervals from stated precision.","Find bounds for products, quotients, powers and contextual quantities.","Calculate absolute and percentage error and evaluate the reliability of a conclusion."],"vocab":["exact value","estimate","approximation","decimal places","significant figures","precision","lower bound","upper bound","absolute error","percentage error","guard digit"],"technology":"A calculator displays digits, not certainty. Use full precision internally, record measurement bounds explicitly and round only the final result to a justified level.","source_basis":["Nikolaidis Topic 1A — rounding, scientific form and percentage error","Supplied MAI 1.1 numbers, rounding and percentage-error exercise set","Pearson AI SL — approximations, bounds and percentage error"],"syllabus_focus":"Unit 1 — rounding, significant figures, bounds, absolute error and percentage error.","slides":[{"section":"Launch","title":"1.6 · Approximation, Bounds and Percentage Error","html":"<div class=\"u1-cover\"><div><div class=\"u1-kicker\">IB DP Mathematics · Applications and Interpretation SL</div><h1><span>1.6</span>Approximation, Bounds and Percentage Error</h1><p class=\"u1-cover-lead\">Communicate precision honestly, construct half-open bounds, propagate measurement uncertainty and judge the significance of error.</p><div class=\"u1-pill-row\"><span>36 purposeful screens</span><span>52 Practice Studio questions</span><span>14-question quiz</span><span>3 IB tasks</span></div><div class=\"u1-central-idea\"><b>Central idea</b><p>A reported number is evidence about an interval, not necessarily an exact value. Good mathematics tracks what is known, what is rounded and how uncertainty changes through a calculation.</p></div><button class=\"route-jump u1-start\" data-go=\"learn\" data-cover-next=\"1\">Begin lesson →</button></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A reported value represents an interval\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">A reported value represents an interval</text>\n    <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"145\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"145\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n    <line x1=\"475\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"475\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"145\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"475\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n    <path d=\"M155 175 H465\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"10\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <text x=\"145\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">2.25</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">reported 2.30</text>\n    <text x=\"475\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">2.35</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"235\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">lower included · upper excluded</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">precision determines the interval width</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"cover","eyebrow":"Unit 1 · Number and Algebra"},{"section":"Launch","title":"A question worth investigating","html":"<div class=\"u1-launch\"><div><blockquote>How many digits should we trust in a calculated answer when every measurement has limited precision?</blockquote><p>The same one-centimetre measuring precision creates a tiny relative uncertainty for a person’s height but a large relative uncertainty for a short piece of paper. Error must be judged relative to scale.</p><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"launch-inquiry\" aria-label=\"Initial inquiry response\"></textarea></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Same absolute error, different significance\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Same absolute error, different significance</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">person height</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">0.5 cm of ≈176 cm</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">about 0.285%</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">paper length</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">0.5 cm of ≈12 cm</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">about 4.3%</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">percentage error measures relative significance</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"inquiry","eyebrow":"Think · discuss · revisit"},{"section":"Learning route","title":"Learning intentions and evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-goals\"><div class=\"u1-goal-grid\"><article><b>1</b><p>Round appropriately to decimal places, significant figures and practical units.</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Distinguish exact values, estimates, approximations and calculator displays.</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Construct half-open intervals from stated precision.</p></article><article><b>4</b><p>Find bounds for products, quotients, powers and contextual quantities.</p></article><article><b>5</b><p>Calculate absolute and percentage error and evaluate the reliability of a conclusion.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-evidence\"><h3>Evidence of understanding</h3><ul><li>Identify the rounding unit and half-unit correctly, including significant-figure cases.</li><li>Use lower and upper endpoints according to monotonicity of the expression.</li><li>Retain guard digits until the final justified rounding stage.</li><li>Interpret percentage error relative to the exact or reference value, with units and context.</li></ul></div></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Know what success looks like"},{"section":"Diagnostic","title":"Try before instruction","html":"<div class=\"u1-diagnostic\"><div><span>Diagnostic</span><p>A ruler reports \\(L=2.3\\) cm correct to 0.1 cm. Write the full interval of possible values and explain why one endpoint is included while the other is not.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"diagnostic\"></textarea><details><summary>Diagnostic lens</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Expected \\(2.25\\le L<2.35\\). The lower endpoint rounds to 2.3; the upper endpoint would round to 2.4 under the standard convention.</p></div></details></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"2.3 cm correct to 0.1 cm\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">2.3 cm correct to 0.1 cm</text>\n    <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"145\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"145\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n    <line x1=\"475\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"475\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"145\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"475\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n    <path d=\"M155 175 H465\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"10\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <text x=\"145\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">2.25</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">reported 2.30</text>\n    <text x=\"475\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">2.35</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"235\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">lower included · upper excluded</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">closed at the lower bound · open at the upper bound</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Use errors as information"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Decimal places, significant figures and meaningful reporting","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Decimal places count positions after the decimal point. Significant figures count from the first non-zero digit and communicate scale-sensitive precision. Practical contexts may require currency, whole objects or a stated IB accuracy convention.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[\\text{rounding unit}=10^k\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\text{maximum rounding error}=\\frac{10^k}{2}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>A trailing zero after a decimal point can be significant because it identifies the last reported place.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Decimal places, significant figures and meaningful reporting\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Decimal places, significant figures and meaningful reporting</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"118.75\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">number</text><rect x=\"182.5\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"246.25\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">2 d.p.</text><rect x=\"310.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"373.75\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">3 s.f.</text><rect x=\"437.5\" y=\"82\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"501.25\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">meaning</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"118.75\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">0.04362018</text><rect x=\"182.5\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"246.25\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">0.04</text><rect x=\"310.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"373.75\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">0.0436</text><rect x=\"437.5\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"501.25\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">different last places</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"118.75\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">12348</text><rect x=\"182.5\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"246.25\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">12348.00</text><rect x=\"310.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"373.75\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">12300</text><rect x=\"437.5\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"127.5\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"501.25\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">scale-sensitive rounding</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">decimal places and significant figures answer different questions</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 1 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Worked example · small-number rounding","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Round \\(0.04362018\\) to 3 decimal places, 3 significant figures and 5 significant figures.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Three decimal places</span><p>The thousandths digit is 3 and the next digit is 6, giving \\(0.044\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Three significant figures</span><p>Count from 4: \\(0.0436\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Five significant figures</span><p>\\(0.043620\\); the final zero communicates the fifth significant place.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The same original value can produce different approximations because the requested precision differs.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Round \\(1352.406\\) to 2 d.p., 3 s.f. and 2 s.f.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-1\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>2 d.p.: \\(1352.41\\). 3 s.f.: \\(1350\\). 2 s.f.: \\(1400\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>For decimal places, work from the decimal point. For significant figures, start at the first non-zero digit and preserve place value with zeros.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why can the value 4.70 communicate more measurement precision than 4.7?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Leading zeros in 0.00450 are significant.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>Leading zeros locate the decimal point; the significant digits are 4, 5 and the trailing 0.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Begin counting significant figures at the first non-zero digit.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>How many significant figures are in 0.03040?</p><p><b>Check:</b> Four: 3, 0, 4, 0.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Exact values, estimates and calculator displays","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>An exact expression such as \\(\\sqrt{65}\\) or \\(2\\pi\\) contains no rounding. A calculator decimal is an approximation. Estimation rounds inputs to support mental checking, while final rounding communicates a justified level of accuracy.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[\\text{estimate}\\longrightarrow\\text{full calculation}\\longrightarrow\\text{validated rounded result}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>“Exact” describes representation; “accurate” describes closeness to a reference or true value.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Exact values, estimates and calculator displays\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Exact values, estimates and calculator displays</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">exact</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">√65</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">symbolic value</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">approximate</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">8.06 to 3 s.f.</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">rounded decimal</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">use both forms when the context requires them</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 2 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Worked example · estimate, calculate and report","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>A wall is 8.11 m by 3.82 m. Estimate its area, calculate the full value and report to 2 d.p.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Estimate</span><p>\\(8\\times4=32\\text{ m}^2\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Calculate</span><p>\\(8.11\\times3.82=30.9802\\text{ m}^2\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Report</span><p>\\(30.98\\text{ m}^2\\) to 2 d.p.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Validate</span><p>The exact calculation is reasonably close to 32.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">An estimate is a reasonableness check, not a substitute for the required calculation.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>A circular area is \\(10\\text{ cm}^2\\). Give the radius exactly and to 3 s.f.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-2\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(r=\\sqrt{10/\\pi}\\text{ cm}\\) exactly, and \\(r\\approx1.78\\text{ cm}\\) to 3 s.f.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Use \\(A=\\pi r^2\\), so \\(r=\\sqrt{A/\\pi}\\). Keep the exact expression until a decimal approximation is requested.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why does a long calculator display not make measured inputs exact?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Every digit shown by the calculator is meaningful.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>The algorithm can produce many digits even when the inputs are measured only roughly.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Base final reporting on input precision, model quality and the question requirement.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Should a cost be reported as QAR 39.8300000?</p><p><b>Check:</b> No. Currency is normally reported to two decimal places unless the context says otherwise.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Constructing half-open bounds","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>If a value is reported to the nearest unit \\(p\\), the true value lies from half a unit below, inclusive, to half a unit above, exclusive. For significant figures, first identify the place value of the last significant digit.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[x-\\frac p2\\le X<x+\\frac p2\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>The upper endpoint is excluded because it would round to the next reported value.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Constructing half-open bounds\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Constructing half-open bounds</text>\n    <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"540\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"5\"/>\n    <line x1=\"145\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"145\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n    <line x1=\"475\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"475\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"145\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"475\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n    <path d=\"M155 175 H465\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"10\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <text x=\"145\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">42.25</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">reported 42.30</text>\n    <text x=\"475\" y=\"125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">42.35</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"235\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">lower included · upper excluded</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">42.3 kg correct to 0.1 kg</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 3 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Worked example · significant-figure bounds","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>The value of \\(x\\) correct to 3 s.f. is 34500. Find the interval of possible values.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Locate the last significant place</span><p>In 34500 to 3 s.f., the last significant digit is the hundreds digit.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Set the rounding unit</span><p>\\(p=100\\), so half is 50.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Write the interval</span><p>\\(34450\\le x<34550\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Zeros preserve place value even when they are not all significant.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>The value of \\(y\\) correct to 3 s.f. is \\(0.0301\\). Find its bounds.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-3\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>The last significant digit is in the ten-thousandths place, so \\(0.03005\\le y<0.03015\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The significant digits are 3, 0 and 1. The rounding unit is \\(0.0001\\), so the half-unit is \\(0.00005\\).</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">How can you determine the rounding unit without guessing from the number of zeros?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“For 145 correct to 3 s.f., use 144.5 to 145.5; for every 3-s.f. number the half-unit is 0.5.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>The half-unit depends on the place of the last significant digit, not the number of significant figures alone.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Mark the last retained significant digit and identify its place value.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>What is the half-unit for 1.45×10⁶ to 3 s.f.?</p><p><b>Check:</b> 5000, because the last significant digit is in the ten-thousands place.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Bounds through calculations","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>For positive inputs, products increase when either input increases; quotients increase with the numerator and decrease with the denominator. Powers and roots are handled using their monotonic behavior on the relevant domain.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[A_{\\min}=L_{\\min}W_{\\min},\\quad A_{\\max}=L_{\\max}W_{\\max},\\quad \\left(\\frac xy\\right)_{\\max}=\\frac{x_{\\max}}{y_{\\min}}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Choose endpoints from the behavior of the expression, not by always pairing “lower with lower.”</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bounds through calculations\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Bounds through calculations</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">minimum quotient</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">small numerator / large denominator</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">x_min / y_max</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">maximum quotient</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">large numerator / small denominator</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">x_max / y_min</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">monotonicity determines endpoint choices</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 4 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Worked example · BMI interval","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>A person has mass 65.5 kg correct to 0.1 kg and height 1.76 m correct to 0.01 m. Find BMI bounds.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Input bounds</span><p>\\(65.45\\le M<65.55\\), \\(1.755\\le h<1.765\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Minimum BMI</span><p>\\(65.45/1.765^2\\approx21.010\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Maximum BMI</span><p>\\(65.55/1.755^2\\approx21.282\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>State interval</span><p>Approximately \\(21.01\\le BMI<21.29\\text{ kg m}^{-2}\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The denominator is squared, so height uncertainty has amplified influence.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>A speed is distance divided by time. Distance is 120 m correct to 1 m and time is 15.0 s correct to 0.1 s. Find speed bounds.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-4\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(119.5\\le d<120.5\\), \\(14.95\\le t<15.05\\). Thus \\(v_{\\min}=119.5/15.05\\approx7.94\\) and \\(v_{\\max}=120.5/14.95\\approx8.06\\text{ m s}^{-1}\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The minimum quotient uses the smallest distance and largest time. The maximum uses the largest distance and smallest time. Preserve the half-open interpretation at the upper endpoint.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why does “lower with lower, upper with upper” fail for quotients?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Always combine both lower bounds for the minimum and both upper bounds for the maximum.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>That works for positive products but not for quotients, where the denominator has the opposite effect.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Ask whether increasing each variable increases or decreases the expression.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>How do you maximize \\(x^2/y\\) for positive bounded \\(x,y\\)?</p><p><b>Check:</b> Use maximum \\(x\\) and minimum \\(y\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Absolute error, percentage error and decision quality","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Absolute error records the size of a difference in the original units. Percentage error divides by the exact or reference value, allowing errors at different scales to be compared. In measurement bounds, the largest percentage error may occur at the smaller exact endpoint.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[E_{\\mathrm{abs}}=|v_A-v_E|,\\qquad E_\\%=\\frac{|v_A-v_E|}{|v_E|}\\times100\\%\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>State which value is treated as exact or reference; percentage error is meaningless without that denominator.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Absolute error, percentage error and decision quality\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Absolute error, percentage error and decision quality</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">A=1003 → 1000</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">absolute error 3</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">≈0.299%</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">B=1 000 003 → 1 000 000</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">absolute error 3</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">≈0.000300%</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">same absolute error · very different relative significance</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 5 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Worked example · maximum percentage error of a measurement","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>A metre stick reports 12 cm correct to 1 cm. Find the magnitude of the maximum percentage error.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Construct the interval</span><p>\\(11.5\\le L<12.5\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Compare endpoint deviations</span><p>The absolute deviation from 12 is 0.5 cm at either side.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Use the smaller exact denominator</span><p>\\(0.5/11.5\\times100\\approx4.348\\%\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Interpret</span><p>The reading can differ from the true length by about 4.35% in the worst case.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Relative error is larger when the same absolute uncertainty is compared with a smaller quantity.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>The exact value is \\(0.1265\\) and it is reported to 2 s.f. Find the percentage error.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-5\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>The reported value is \\(0.13\\). Percentage error \\(=|0.13-0.1265|/0.1265\\times100\\approx2.767\\%\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The exact value belongs in the denominator. Rounding the error too early would distort the final percentage, so retain guard digits.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why is an error of 3 units not equally serious for values near 1000 and near one million?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Percentage error divides by the approximate value.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>The standard comparison measures deviation relative to the exact or accepted reference value.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Label \\(v_E\\) and \\(v_A\\) before substituting.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Exact 50, approximate 48: what is the percentage error?</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(2/50\\times100=4\\%\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Interactive investigation","html":"<div class=\"u1-lab-intro\"><div><h3>Predict first</h3><p>Change two reported values and their rounding units. Predict the first interval and the resulting product interval before using the calculator.</p><p class=\"u1-tech-note\">A calculator displays digits, not certainty. Use full precision internally, record measurement bounds explicitly and round only the final result to a justified level.</p></div><div class=\"lab\" id=\"lesson-lab\" data-lab=\"1.6\"></div></div>","kind":"lab","eyebrow":"Technology must expose structure"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Transparent GDC protocol","html":"<div class=\"u1-protocol\"><article><b>1</b><h3>Identify precision</h3><p>Determine the smallest reported unit for every measured input.</p></article><article><b>2</b><h3>Construct bounds</h3><p>Use reported value ± half the precision in a half-open interval.</p></article><article><b>3</b><h3>Choose extrema</h3><p>Use monotonicity to select the endpoint combination for a minimum or maximum.</p></article><article><b>4</b><h3>Calculate</h3><p>Retain guard digits and preserve units.</p></article><article><b>5</b><h3>Communicate</h3><p>Report an interval or suitable rounded result and explain its reliability.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-callout\"><b>IB communication:</b> record the model, relevant settings or entries, output, units and interpretation.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Use technology, but show mathematics"},{"section":"Synthesis","title":"Mixed reasoning challenge","html":"<div class=\"u1-synthesis\"><div class=\"u1-synthesis-grid\"><article><b>A</b><p>A rectangular sign is measured as 64.3 cm by 48.2 cm, each correct to 0.1 cm.</p></article><article><b>B</b><p>Find the bounds for both dimensions and for the area.</p></article><article><b>C</b><p>Compare the area interval width with the original measurement precision.</p></article><article><b>D</b><p>Explain why reporting the area to six decimal places would be misleading.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"synthesis\"></textarea><details><summary>Solution architecture</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(64.25\\le L<64.35\\) and \\(48.15\\le W<48.25\\). Since both dimensions are positive, the minimum area is \\(64.25(48.15)\\approx3093.64\\text{ cm}^2\\) and the upper endpoint is \\(64.35(48.25)\\approx3104.89\\text{ cm}^2\\). The area uncertainty spans about 11.25 cm², much larger than 0.1 in its own units, so excessive decimal reporting would imply unsupported precision.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Connect representations and constraints"},{"section":"IB-style task","title":"Extended-response preview","html":"<div class=\"u1-task\"><div class=\"u1-task-meta\"><span>Paper 2 · measurement model</span><span>10 marks</span><span>GDC expected</span></div><h3>BMI bounds</h3><p>A person has mass 65.5 kg correct to 0.1 kg and height 1.76 m correct to 0.01 m.</p><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(a)</b><p>Write bounds for mass and height.</p><span>[2]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(b)</b><p>Find minimum and maximum BMI.</p><span>[4]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(c)</b><p>Explain the endpoint choices.</p><span>[2]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(d)</b><p>Evaluate one limitation of BMI as a health indicator.</p><span>[2]</span></div><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"exam\">Open all 3 IB tasks</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Read the command term and the context"},{"section":"Assessment literacy","title":"How marks are earned","html":"<div class=\"u1-command-grid\"><article><b>State</b><p>Give a concise bound, value or fact.</p></article><article><b>Calculate</b><p>Show substitutions and report suitable accuracy.</p></article><article><b>Find</b><p>Select and carry out a valid method.</p></article><article><b>Explain</b><p>Connect endpoint choices to monotonicity or rounding.</p></article><article><b>Compare</b><p>Use a common measure such as percentage error.</p></article><article><b>Evaluate</b><p>Judge reliability and contextual limitations.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-mark-chain\"><span>model</span><i>→</i><span>method</span><i>→</i><span>accuracy</span><i>→</i><span>interpretation</span><i>→</i><span>evaluation</span></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Write for mathematical evidence"},{"section":"Exit ticket","title":"Three pieces of evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-exit-wrap\"><div class=\"u1-exit\"><article><b>1</b><p>Write the bounds for \\(0.0301\\) correct to 3 s.f.</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>A square side is 7.0 cm correct to 0.1 cm. Find area bounds.</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Explain why trailing zeros can communicate precision.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"exit-ticket\"></textarea><details><summary>Teacher check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Expected \\(0.03005\\le x<0.03015\\); \\(6.95^2\\le A<7.05^2\\), so \\(48.3025\\le A<49.7025\\text{ cm}^2\\); in a decimal measurement, trailing zeros can show the last measured place.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Independent check"},{"section":"Reflection","title":"Where this idea travels next","html":"<div class=\"u1-connections\"><article><b>Science</b><p>Measurement uncertainty is essential in experimental conclusions.</p></article><article><b>Geometry</b><p>Area, volume and trigonometric calculations propagate input bounds.</p></article><article><b>Statistics</b><p>Accuracy, precision and sampling variability are different concepts.</p></article><article><b>Modelling</b><p>A model result cannot be more trustworthy than its data and assumptions.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-reflection\"><h3>Return to the inquiry</h3><p>Which digits in a calculator output are mathematically computed, and which are actually supported by the quality of the input data?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"reflection\"></textarea></div><div class=\"u1-route-launch\"><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"practice\">Practice Studio</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"quiz\">Timed Quiz</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"review\">Mastery evidence</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Learn → practise → demonstrate → review"}]},"1.7":{"number":"1.7","title":"Loans, Annuities and Amortization","subtitle":"Model repeated cash flows, use TVM variables transparently, and compare repayment or saving strategies with evidence.","objectives":["Represent equal periodic cash flows on a timeline and distinguish ordinary annuities from annuities due.","Calculate future values of savings annuities and periodic loan payments.","Use TVM variables and cash-flow signs consistently.","Construct and interpret an amortization schedule and outstanding balance.","Compare financial strategies using total interest, term, liquidity and assumptions."],"vocab":["cash flow","ordinary annuity","annuity due","future value","present value","periodic payment","amortization","principal component","interest component","outstanding balance"],"technology":"Use TVM/finance tools only after recording N, I%, PV, PMT, FV and payment timing. Maintain a consistent sign convention and verify one period using balance ×(1+i) plus or minus the payment.","source_basis":["Pearson AI SL — financial applications of sequences and TVM methods","Finite geometric-series structure from the supplied sequence references","Original ECHS loan, saving and amortization tasks"],"syllabus_focus":"Unit 1 — savings annuities, loan payments, amortization and financial decision-making.","slides":[{"section":"Launch","title":"1.7 · Loans, Annuities and Amortization","html":"<div class=\"u1-cover\"><div><div class=\"u1-kicker\">IB DP Mathematics · Applications and Interpretation SL</div><h1><span>1.7</span>Loans, Annuities and Amortization</h1><p class=\"u1-cover-lead\">Model repeated cash flows, use TVM variables transparently, and compare repayment or saving strategies with evidence.</p><div class=\"u1-pill-row\"><span>36 purposeful screens</span><span>52 Practice Studio questions</span><span>14-question quiz</span><span>3 IB tasks</span></div><div class=\"u1-central-idea\"><b>Central idea</b><p>An annuity is a geometric accumulation of equal cash flows. Timing, sign convention and periodic rates determine whether a financial calculator result represents saving, borrowing or repayment.</p></div><button class=\"route-jump u1-start\" data-go=\"learn\" data-cover-next=\"1\">Begin lesson →</button></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Equal payments, unequal accumulation times\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Equal payments, unequal accumulation times</text>\n    <line x1=\"70\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><line x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M82.0 139 L90.0 122 L98.0 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"90.0\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">PMT</text><text x=\"90.0\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=1</text><line x1=\"236.7\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"236.7\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M228.7 139 L236.7 122 L244.7 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"236.7\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">PMT</text><text x=\"236.7\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=2</text><line x1=\"383.3\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"383.3\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M375.3 139 L383.3 122 L391.3 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"383.3\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">PMT</text><text x=\"383.3\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=3</text><line x1=\"530.0\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"530.0\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M522.0 139 L530.0 122 L538.0 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"530.0\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">PMT</text><text x=\"530.0\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=4</text><path d=\"M105 260 Q310 295 515 260\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 8\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">each payment accumulates for a different time</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">each cash flow has its own exponent</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"cover","eyebrow":"Unit 1 · Number and Algebra"},{"section":"Launch","title":"A question worth investigating","html":"<div class=\"u1-launch\"><div><blockquote>Why can two loans with the same principal and annual rate have very different total costs?</blockquote><p>A longer loan reduces each monthly payment but may greatly increase total interest. A financially “affordable” payment is not automatically the least expensive decision.</p><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"launch-inquiry\" aria-label=\"Initial inquiry response\"></textarea></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Term trade-off\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Term trade-off</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">longer term</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">lower monthly payment</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">more periods of interest</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">shorter term</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">higher monthly payment</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">lower total interest</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">affordability and total cost are different objectives</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"inquiry","eyebrow":"Think · discuss · revisit"},{"section":"Learning route","title":"Learning intentions and evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-goals\"><div class=\"u1-goal-grid\"><article><b>1</b><p>Represent equal periodic cash flows on a timeline and distinguish ordinary annuities from annuities due.</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Calculate future values of savings annuities and periodic loan payments.</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Use TVM variables and cash-flow signs consistently.</p></article><article><b>4</b><p>Construct and interpret an amortization schedule and outstanding balance.</p></article><article><b>5</b><p>Compare financial strategies using total interest, term, liquidity and assumptions.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-evidence\"><h3>Evidence of understanding</h3><ul><li>Draw or describe the cash-flow timing before selecting a formula or TVM settings.</li><li>Synchronize nominal annual rates with payment frequency.</li><li>Verify a calculator payment by checking one balance recurrence.</li><li>Interpret principal, interest, outstanding balance and total cost separately.</li></ul></div></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Know what success looks like"},{"section":"Diagnostic","title":"Try before instruction","html":"<div class=\"u1-diagnostic\"><div><span>Diagnostic</span><p>A saver deposits QAR 400 at the end of each month into an account earning 4.8% nominal compounded monthly. Which deposit earns interest for the longest time, and why can the total not be modelled as \\(400n(1+i)^n\\)?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"diagnostic\"></textarea><details><summary>Diagnostic lens</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>The first deposit earns interest for the most periods; each deposit has a different accumulation time. The future value is a geometric sum of shifted cash flows, not one lump sum invested for the full term.</p></div></details></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Ordinary savings annuity\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Ordinary savings annuity</text>\n    <line x1=\"70\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><line x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M82.0 139 L90.0 122 L98.0 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"90.0\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">400</text><text x=\"90.0\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=1</text><line x1=\"236.7\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"236.7\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M228.7 139 L236.7 122 L244.7 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"236.7\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">400</text><text x=\"236.7\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=2</text><line x1=\"383.3\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"383.3\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M375.3 139 L383.3 122 L391.3 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"383.3\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">400</text><text x=\"383.3\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=3</text><line x1=\"530.0\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"530.0\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M522.0 139 L530.0 122 L538.0 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"530.0\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">400</text><text x=\"530.0\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=4</text><path d=\"M105 260 Q310 295 515 260\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 8\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">each payment accumulates for a different time</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">deposits occur at period ends</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Use errors as information"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Cash-flow timelines and payment timing","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Equal payments do not all experience the same number of compounding periods. In an ordinary annuity, payments occur at the end of each period. In an annuity due, each payment occurs one period earlier and therefore accumulates for one additional period.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[\\text{annuity due FV}=(1+i)\\times\\text{ordinary annuity FV}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>A cash-flow diagram is part of the model, not decoration.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cash-flow timelines and payment timing\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Cash-flow timelines and payment timing</text>\n    <line x1=\"70\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"550\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><line x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M82.0 139 L90.0 122 L98.0 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"90.0\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">R</text><text x=\"90.0\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=1</text><line x1=\"236.7\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"236.7\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M228.7 139 L236.7 122 L244.7 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"236.7\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">R</text><text x=\"236.7\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=2</text><line x1=\"383.3\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"383.3\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M375.3 139 L383.3 122 L391.3 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"383.3\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">R</text><text x=\"383.3\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=3</text><line x1=\"530.0\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"530.0\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M522.0 139 L530.0 122 L538.0 139\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"530.0\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#7a1733\">R</text><text x=\"530.0\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">t=4</text><path d=\"M105 260 Q310 295 515 260\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 8\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">each payment accumulates for a different time</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">ordinary annuity: first payment grows longest, last payment grows zero periods</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 1 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Worked example · expand before using a formula","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Three deposits of QAR 1000 are made at the end of years 1, 2 and 3 at 5% annual interest. Find the value immediately after the third deposit.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Track deposit 1</span><p>It grows for two years: \\(1000(1.05)^2\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Track deposit 2</span><p>It grows for one year: \\(1000(1.05)\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Track deposit 3</span><p>It has just been deposited: \\(1000\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Add</span><p>Total \\(=1000[(1.05)^2+1.05+1]=3152.50\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Expanding a small case reveals the geometric structure and timing.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>How would the value change if the same three deposits were made at the beginning of each year?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-1\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Every deposit grows for one additional year, so multiply the ordinary-annuity value by 1.05: QAR \\(3310.13\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The beginning deposits occur one period earlier. Their exponents become 3, 2 and 1 rather than 2, 1 and 0.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why is the last ordinary-annuity deposit not multiplied by an interest factor at the valuation time?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“All deposits can be combined as \\(nR(1+i)^n\\).”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>That treats every deposit as if it were invested for the full term.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Draw the timeline and assign a separate exponent to each cash flow.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>For four end-of-year deposits valued immediately after year 4, what are the exponents?</p><p><b>Check:</b> 3, 2, 1 and 0.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Future value of an ordinary annuity","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Factoring equal payments from the accumulated cash flows produces a finite geometric sum. The ordinary-annuity future value formula assumes equal payments, equal periods, a constant periodic rate and end-of-period timing.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[FV=R\\frac{(1+i)^n-1}{i}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>\\(R\\) is the periodic payment, \\(i\\) is the periodic rate and \\(n\\) is the number of payments.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Future value of an ordinary annuity\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Future value of an ordinary annuity</text>\n    <defs><marker id=\"tarr\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L7,3 z\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"175\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><circle cx=\"70.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#7a1733\"/><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">payment 1</text><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">R(1+i)ⁿ⁻¹</text><path d=\"M84.0 155 Q150.0 90 216.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"150.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">accumulate</text><circle cx=\"230.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><text x=\"230.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">payment 2</text><text x=\"230.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">R(1+i)ⁿ⁻²</text><path d=\"M244.0 155 Q310.0 90 376.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">accumulate</text><circle cx=\"390.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#17324d\"/><text x=\"390.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">…</text><text x=\"390.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">…</text><path d=\"M404.0 155 Q470.0 90 536.0 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#tarr)\"/><text x=\"470.0\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">sum</text><circle cx=\"550.0\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#177e89\"/><text x=\"550.0\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">payment n</text><text x=\"550.0\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">R</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">factor R and recognize a finite geometric series</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 2 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Worked example · monthly saving","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>QAR 400 is deposited monthly for 10 years at 4.8% nominal compounded monthly. Find the future value.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Periodic rate</span><p>\\(i=0.048/12=0.004\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Payment count</span><p>\\(n=120\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Apply formula</span><p>\\(FV=400\\frac{(1.004)^{120}-1}{0.004}\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Calculate</span><p>\\(FV\\approx\\text{QAR }61452.78\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The total deposits are QAR 48 000; the excess is investment growth.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Find the future value of monthly deposits of QAR 120 for four years at 3.6% nominal compounded monthly.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-2\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(i=0.003,n=48\\). \\(FV=120[(1.003)^{48}-1]/0.003\\approx\\text{QAR }6185.41\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>There are 48 end-of-month payments. The nominal annual rate is divided by 12 before the formula is used.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">How can you separate the amount deposited from the interest earned?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Use the annual rate 0.036 with 48 monthly payments.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>The rate period and payment period would be inconsistent.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Convert both rate and count to months.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>What periodic rate matches 6% nominal with quarterly payments?</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(0.06/4=0.015\\) per quarter.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Loan payment and present value","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>A loan payment formula equates the borrowed principal with the present value of the future repayment annuity. The periodic payment covers current interest and reduces principal. A TVM solver uses opposite signs for money received and money paid.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[PMT=P\\frac{i}{1-(1+i)^{-n}}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Payment frequency, compounding frequency and rate period must agree.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Loan payment and present value\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Loan payment and present value</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">at time 0</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">receive +P</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">loan principal</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">future periods</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">pay −PMT repeatedly</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">repayment annuity</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">opposite signs encode opposite cash-flow directions</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 3 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Worked example · monthly loan payment","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Find the monthly payment on QAR 280 000 over 20 years at 4.2% nominal compounded monthly.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Periodic rate</span><p>\\(i=0.042/12=0.0035\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Periods</span><p>\\(n=240\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Formula</span><p>\\(PMT=280000\\frac{0.0035}{1-(1.0035)^{-240}}\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Calculate</span><p>\\(PMT\\approx\\text{QAR }1726.40\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The payment is positive as a magnitude; a TVM sign convention may display it as negative.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Find the monthly payment on QAR 85 000 over six years at 5.2% nominal compounded monthly.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-3\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(i=0.052/12,n=72\\). The payment is approximately QAR \\(1376.82\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Use the periodic rate and 72 monthly payments. A rough check is that \\(85000/72\\approx1181\\), so the payment must be somewhat larger to include interest.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why can a lower monthly payment correspond to a higher total cost?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“PV and PMT should have the same sign because both refer to the same loan.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>One is money received and the other is money paid; they are opposite cash-flow directions.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Choose a viewpoint and apply signs consistently.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>From the borrower’s viewpoint, what signs should PV and PMT have?</p><p><b>Check:</b> PV positive (received), PMT negative (paid), or the exact opposite convention if used consistently.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Amortization and outstanding balance","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Each period begins with an outstanding balance. Interest is calculated on that balance, then the payment reduces the account. Early payments often contain more interest because the balance is larger.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[I_k=iB_{k-1},\\qquad B_k=B_{k-1}+I_k-PMT\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Payment = interest component + principal component.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Amortization and outstanding balance\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Amortization and outstanding balance</text>\n    <rect x=\"85\" y=\"105.0\" width=\"58\" height=\"130.0\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#7a1733\" opacity=\".9\"/><text x=\"114\" y=\"258\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">P0</text><text x=\"114\" y=\"95.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">240000</text><rect x=\"185\" y=\"105.8\" width=\"58\" height=\"129.2\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\".9\"/><text x=\"214\" y=\"258\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">P1</text><text x=\"214\" y=\"95.8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">238500</text><rect x=\"285\" y=\"106.7\" width=\"58\" height=\"128.3\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\".9\"/><text x=\"314\" y=\"258\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">P2</text><text x=\"314\" y=\"96.7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">236950</text><rect x=\"385\" y=\"107.5\" width=\"58\" height=\"127.5\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\".9\"/><text x=\"414\" y=\"258\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">P3</text><text x=\"414\" y=\"97.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">235350</text><rect x=\"485\" y=\"108.4\" width=\"58\" height=\"126.6\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#177e89\" opacity=\".9\"/><text x=\"514\" y=\"258\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#17324d\">P4</text><text x=\"514\" y=\"98.4\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">233700</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">as balance falls, the interest component usually falls</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 4 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Worked example · one amortization row","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>A loan balance is QAR 100 000, the monthly rate is 0.4%, and the payment is QAR 1500. Find the next balance and payment components.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Interest</span><p>\\(I=100000(0.004)=400\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Principal repaid</span><p>\\(1500-400=1100\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>New balance</span><p>\\(100000-1100=98900\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Check recurrence</span><p>\\(100000(1.004)-1500=98900\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The two calculation paths must agree.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>For a balance of QAR 98 900 with the same rate and payment, find the next month’s interest, principal reduction and balance.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-4\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Interest \\(=98900(0.004)=395.60\\). Principal reduction \\(=1104.40\\). New balance \\(=97795.60\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The interest component falls because the balance is smaller, so more of the unchanged payment reduces principal.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why does an extra principal payment reduce more than the balance at that moment?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Every payment contains the same amount of interest.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>Interest is a percentage of the changing balance, so it usually decreases over time in a fixed-payment loan.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Calculate interest from the opening balance of each period.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>What happens to the principal component as interest falls but payment stays fixed?</p><p><b>Check:</b> The principal component increases.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Comparing repayment strategies and model assumptions","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>A shorter term normally raises periodic payments but reduces the number of interest periods. Extra payments made early can have a larger effect than the same amount paid late. A complete comparison also considers fees, rate changes and liquidity.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[\\text{total interest}=n(PMT)-P\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>“Affordable” describes a constraint; “least total cost” describes a different objective.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Comparing repayment strategies and model assumptions\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Comparing repayment strategies and model assumptions</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">25-year term</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">lower payment</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">more total interest</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">20-year term</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">higher payment</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">less total interest</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">state the decision objective before ranking options</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 5 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Worked example · term comparison","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>For a QAR 240 000 loan at 3.9% nominal monthly, compare 20-year and 25-year terms.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Compute each payment</span><p>Use \\(i=0.039/12\\) with \\(n=240\\) and \\(n=300\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Compute total paid</span><p>Multiply each payment by its number of periods.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Compute total interest</span><p>Subtract the principal from each total.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Evaluate</span><p>The 20-year option costs less overall but demands a higher monthly payment.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">A recommendation should state both the mathematical winner and the borrower constraint.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>A borrower can pay an extra QAR 5000 now or after five years. Which timing reduces more interest, assuming no penalty and the rate is unchanged?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-5\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Paying now reduces more interest because the principal is lower for every intervening period.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Early principal reduction removes future interest on that amount and on the interest that would have accumulated from it. This is a compounding effect.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">What assumptions in a fixed-rate amortization calculation may fail in reality?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Choose the option with the smallest monthly payment.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>That ignores the number of payments, total interest and the opportunity cost of a longer debt period.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Compare payment, total cost, term and financial resilience together.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>When might the longer term still be reasonable?</p><p><b>Check:</b> When the shorter payment is not affordable or liquidity and risk management justify the higher total cost.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Interactive investigation","html":"<div class=\"u1-lab-intro\"><div><h3>Predict first</h3><p>Vary loan principal, nominal rate, years and payments per year. Predict how payment and total interest respond, then compare a shorter and longer term.</p><p class=\"u1-tech-note\">Use TVM/finance tools only after recording N, I%, PV, PMT, FV and payment timing. Maintain a consistent sign convention and verify one period using balance ×(1+i) plus or minus the payment.</p></div><div class=\"lab\" id=\"lesson-lab\" data-lab=\"1.7\"></div></div>","kind":"lab","eyebrow":"Technology must expose structure"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Transparent GDC protocol","html":"<div class=\"u1-protocol\"><article><b>1</b><h3>Draw cash flows</h3><p>Mark deposits or payments and whether they occur at the beginning or end of periods.</p></article><article><b>2</b><h3>Set periods</h3><p>Convert the rate and term to the payment period.</p></article><article><b>3</b><h3>Choose signs</h3><p>Money received and money paid must use opposite signs.</p></article><article><b>4</b><h3>Solve</h3><p>Use a formula, recurrence or TVM solver and retain full precision.</p></article><article><b>5</b><h3>Verify and compare</h3><p>Check one recurrence, then calculate total paid, interest and strategic trade-offs.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-callout\"><b>IB communication:</b> record the model, relevant settings or entries, output, units and interpretation.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Use technology, but show mathematics"},{"section":"Synthesis","title":"Mixed reasoning challenge","html":"<div class=\"u1-synthesis\"><div class=\"u1-synthesis-grid\"><article><b>A</b><p>A QAR 240 000 loan is repaid monthly over 25 years at 3.9% nominal compounded monthly.</p></article><article><b>B</b><p>Find the monthly payment and total interest if the rate remains fixed.</p></article><article><b>C</b><p>Find the balance after 12 payments using the recurrence or remaining-loan formula.</p></article><article><b>D</b><p>Compare with a 20-year term and discuss payment affordability versus total cost.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"synthesis\"></textarea><details><summary>Solution architecture</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>With \\(i=0.039/12\\) and \\(n=300\\), \\(PMT=Pi/[1-(1+i)^{-n}]\\approx\\text{QAR }1253.59\\). Total interest is approximately QAR 136078.30. After 12 payments, apply \\(B_k=P(1+i)^k-PMT\\frac{(1+i)^k-1}{i}\\), giving a balance near QAR 234214.17. A 20-year term raises the payment but lowers total interest substantially.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Connect representations and constraints"},{"section":"IB-style task","title":"Extended-response preview","html":"<div class=\"u1-task\"><div class=\"u1-task-meta\"><span>Paper 2 · TVM and evaluation</span><span>12 marks</span><span>GDC expected</span></div><h3>Family home loan</h3><p>A family borrows QAR 280 000 at 4.2% nominal compounded monthly and considers 20-year and 25-year terms.</p><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(a)</b><p>Find both monthly payments.</p><span>[4]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(b)</b><p>Find total interest under each option.</p><span>[3]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(c)</b><p>Find the balance after two years for one option.</p><span>[3]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(d)</b><p>Evaluate the trade-off.</p><span>[2]</span></div><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"exam\">Open all 3 IB tasks</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Read the command term and the context"},{"section":"Assessment literacy","title":"How marks are earned","html":"<div class=\"u1-command-grid\"><article><b>Calculate</b><p>Use correct TVM inputs or a stated formula.</p></article><article><b>Construct</b><p>Organize an amortization row or schedule with labels.</p></article><article><b>Interpret</b><p>Explain a sign, payment or balance in context.</p></article><article><b>Compare</b><p>Use payment and total cost on a common basis.</p></article><article><b>Evaluate</b><p>Judge trade-offs and assumptions.</p></article><article><b>Verify</b><p>Substitute into a recurrence or check a financial identity.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-mark-chain\"><span>model</span><i>→</i><span>method</span><i>→</i><span>accuracy</span><i>→</i><span>interpretation</span><i>→</i><span>evaluation</span></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Write for mathematical evidence"},{"section":"Exit ticket","title":"Three pieces of evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-exit-wrap\"><div class=\"u1-exit\"><article><b>1</b><p>Find the monthly payment on QAR 85 000 at 5.2% nominal for six years.</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Explain why \\(PV\\) and \\(PMT\\) normally have opposite signs in a TVM solver.</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>State how an extra principal payment changes future interest.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"exit-ticket\"></textarea><details><summary>Teacher check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Expected payment about QAR 1376.82; opposite signs represent receiving the loan and paying it back; reducing principal earlier lowers the balance on which later interest is calculated.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Independent check"},{"section":"Reflection","title":"Where this idea travels next","html":"<div class=\"u1-connections\"><article><b>Geometric series</b><p>Annuity formulas arise from finite geometric sums.</p></article><article><b>Functions</b><p>Outstanding balance is a discrete recurrence and an exponential-expression model.</p></article><article><b>Modelling</b><p>Rates may change and fees may be omitted from a simplified model.</p></article><article><b>Personal finance</b><p>Affordability, risk and liquidity matter alongside total interest.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-reflection\"><h3>Return to the inquiry</h3><p>Which quantities should a borrower compare beyond the advertised annual rate, and how can a repayment schedule reveal hidden trade-offs?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"reflection\"></textarea></div><div class=\"u1-route-launch\"><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"practice\">Practice Studio</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"quiz\">Timed Quiz</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"review\">Mastery evidence</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Learn → practise → demonstrate → review"}]},"1.8":{"number":"1.8","title":"Technology for Equations and Systems","subtitle":"Translate conditions into equations, solve and classify systems with technology, and verify every output against the original model.","objectives":["Model contexts with systems of two or three linear equations.","Solve systems graphically, algebraically or using matrix/equation tools.","Classify unique, inconsistent and dependent systems.","Determine parameters of linear or quadratic models from conditions.","Verify outputs using substitution, residuals, units and contextual constraints."],"vocab":["system of equations","simultaneous solution","unique solution","inconsistent system","dependent system","coefficient matrix","determinant","parameter","root","intersection","residual"],"technology":"Use equation, matrix, graph or numerical-solver modes. Record the system, window or matrix, output and verification. A result is not complete until it is checked against equations and context.","source_basis":["Nikolaidis Topic 1A — two- and three-variable linear systems","Supplied MAI 1.2–1.3 systems-of-linear-equations exercise set","Pearson AI SL — systems, graphs, intersections and technology"],"syllabus_focus":"Unit 1 — technology-based solution and classification of equations and linear systems.","slides":[{"section":"Launch","title":"1.8 · Technology for Equations and Systems","html":"<div class=\"u1-cover\"><div><div class=\"u1-kicker\">IB DP Mathematics · Applications and Interpretation SL</div><h1><span>1.8</span>Technology for Equations and Systems</h1><p class=\"u1-cover-lead\">Translate conditions into equations, solve and classify systems with technology, and verify every output against the original model.</p><div class=\"u1-pill-row\"><span>36 purposeful screens</span><span>52 Practice Studio questions</span><span>14-question quiz</span><span>3 IB tasks</span></div><div class=\"u1-central-idea\"><b>Central idea</b><p>Technology finds numerical solutions; mathematics decides what equations to enter, how many solutions are possible, whether the output is admissible and what it means.</p></div><button class=\"route-jump u1-start\" data-go=\"learn\" data-cover-next=\"1\">Begin lesson →</button></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two equations, one shared solution\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Two equations, one shared solution</text>\n    <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"255\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"295\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"295\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"425\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"425\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"490\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"490\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"555\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"145\" y1=\"225\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><line x1=\"145\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"230\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"329\" cy=\"155\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"345\" y=\"145\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">shared solution</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">an intersection satisfies both equations simultaneously</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"cover","eyebrow":"Unit 1 · Number and Algebra"},{"section":"Launch","title":"A question worth investigating","html":"<div class=\"u1-launch\"><div><blockquote>What can a calculator solve for us, and what mathematical decisions must remain ours?</blockquote><p>A GDC can solve a 3×3 system instantly, but it cannot decide whether a variable should represent a negative price, whether two equations repeat the same information or whether a rounded intersection is reliable.</p><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"launch-inquiry\" aria-label=\"Initial inquiry response\"></textarea></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Technology output versus mathematical conclusion\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Technology output versus mathematical conclusion</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#fff1f3\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"335\" y=\"90\" width=\"230\" height=\"155\" rx=\"22\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#7a1733\">calculator</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">x=4.2, y=3.2</text>\n    <text x=\"170\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">numerical output</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"900\" fill=\"#177e89\">mathematics</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#17324d\">verified, unique, admissible</text>\n    <text x=\"450\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">complete conclusion</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"950\" fill=\"#d4a72c\">↔</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">solve · classify · verify · interpret</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"inquiry","eyebrow":"Think · discuss · revisit"},{"section":"Learning route","title":"Learning intentions and evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-goals\"><div class=\"u1-goal-grid\"><article><b>1</b><p>Model contexts with systems of two or three linear equations.</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Solve systems graphically, algebraically or using matrix/equation tools.</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Classify unique, inconsistent and dependent systems.</p></article><article><b>4</b><p>Determine parameters of linear or quadratic models from conditions.</p></article><article><b>5</b><p>Verify outputs using substitution, residuals, units and contextual constraints.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-evidence\"><h3>Evidence of understanding</h3><ul><li>Define variables and translate each independent condition accurately.</li><li>Choose a method that reveals both the solution and the number of solutions.</li><li>Record technology entries and not only the final decimal output.</li><li>Substitute into every original equation and interpret any rejected solutions.</li></ul></div></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Know what success looks like"},{"section":"Diagnostic","title":"Try before instruction","html":"<div class=\"u1-diagnostic\"><div><span>Diagnostic</span><p>Model and solve: three burgers and five sandwiches cost 21.4 euros; two burgers and three sandwiches cost 13.6 euros. Define variables, write equations and verify the prices.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"diagnostic\"></textarea><details><summary>Diagnostic lens</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Let \\(B,S\\) be prices. Solve \\(3B+5S=21.4\\), \\(2B+3S=13.6\\) to obtain \\(B=3.8,S=2\\). Substitute into both totals and include currency units.</p></div></details></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Translate the purchase data\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Translate the purchase data</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">purchase</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">equation</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"115.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">check</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">3 burgers + 5 sandwiches</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">3B+5S=21.4</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"132.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">3(3.8)+5(2)=21.4</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">2 burgers + 3 sandwiches</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">2B+3S=13.6</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"182.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"50.0\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"215.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">2(3.8)+3(2)=13.6</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">variables carry units and context</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Use errors as information"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Systems as shared solution sets","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>A solution to a system must satisfy every equation simultaneously. For two linear equations, the graphs are lines: one intersection gives a unique solution, parallel distinct lines give no solution, and coincident lines give infinitely many solutions.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[\\begin{cases}a_1x+b_1y=c_1\\\\a_2x+b_2y=c_2\\end{cases}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\text{common points of two lines}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>“Solve the system” means find the intersection of all solution sets, not solve each equation independently.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Systems as shared solution sets\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Systems as shared solution sets</text>\n    <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"255\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"295\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"295\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"425\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"425\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"490\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"490\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"555\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"145\" y1=\"225\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><line x1=\"145\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"230\" stroke=\"#177e89\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"329\" cy=\"155\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#d4a72c\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"345\" y=\"145\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">shared solution</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">one intersection · one ordered-pair solution</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 1 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Worked example · graphical and algebraic agreement","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Solve \\(3x+2y=19\\) and \\(x-y=1\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Use the second equation</span><p>\\(x=y+1\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Substitute</span><p>\\(3(y+1)+2y=19\\Rightarrow5y=16\\Rightarrow y=3.2\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Recover x</span><p>\\(x=4.2\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Verify</span><p>\\(3(4.2)+2(3.2)=19\\) and \\(4.2-3.2=1\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">A graph should show the same intersection, subject to display accuracy.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Solve \\(2x-3y=7\\) and \\(5x+y=4\\), then verify.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-1\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>From \\(y=4-5x\\), substitute: \\(2x-3(4-5x)=7\\Rightarrow17x=19\\), so \\(x=19/17\\), \\(y=-27/17\\). Substitution verifies both.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>The exact fractions preserve accuracy. A decimal graph intersection can be used, but the exact algebraic result gives a stronger verification.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">How does the geometry of the lines reveal the number of solutions before exact calculation?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 1","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Every pair of linear equations has one solution.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>Parallel lines have none; coincident lines have infinitely many.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Compare gradients and intercepts or inspect row-reduction/determinant information.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Classify \\(2x+4y=6\\) and \\(x+2y=3\\).</p><p><b>Check:</b> They are the same line, so there are infinitely many solutions.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Elimination, substitution and matrix structure","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Algebraic methods expose how equations combine. Elimination forms a new equation by adding equivalent multiples; substitution replaces one variable with an equal expression. Matrix or simultaneous-equation mode automates the arithmetic but not the modelling.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},\\qquad \\det A\\ne0\\Rightarrow\\text{unique solution}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Row operations preserve the solution set when applied correctly.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Elimination, substitution and matrix structure\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Elimination, substitution and matrix structure</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">method</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">best when</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">visible evidence</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">substitution</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">one variable is isolated</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">one-variable equation</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">elimination</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">coefficients align</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">cancellation</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">matrix/GDC</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">larger systems</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">coefficient structure</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">choose a method that is efficient and auditable</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 2 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Worked example · eliminate with decimal coefficients","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Solve \\(3.2x-5.6y=-26.4\\) and \\(1.1x+2.7y=23.3\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Enter or scale equations</span><p>Use simultaneous-equation mode or clear decimals consistently.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Solve</span><p>The system gives \\(x=4\\) and \\(y=7\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Verify first equation</span><p>\\(3.2(4)-5.6(7)=12.8-39.2=-26.4\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Verify second equation</span><p>\\(1.1(4)+2.7(7)=4.4+18.9=23.3\\).</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Decimal coefficients are not a reason to skip verification.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Solve \\(5a+7b=19\\), \\(8b-3c=7\\), \\(9a+4c=21\\).</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-2\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Solving the 3×3 system gives \\(a=1,b=2,c=3\\). Substitute: 5+14=19, 16−9=7, 9+12=21.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Organize variables in a consistent order \\((a,b,c)\\), inserting zero coefficients where a variable is absent. This prevents column misalignment in matrix entry.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why must absent variables be represented by zero coefficients in matrix mode?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 2","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Reordering variables differently in each equation does not matter in a matrix.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>Matrix columns must represent the same variable in every row.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Write a labelled coefficient table before entering the matrix.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>What row represents \\(8b-3c=7\\) in \\((a,b,c)\\) order?</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\([0,8,-3\\mid7]\\).</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Classifying unique, inconsistent and dependent systems","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>A determinant of zero signals that a square coefficient matrix is singular, so the system has no unique solution. Further comparison decides whether the equations are inconsistent or dependent. Graphically, this is parallel versus coincident geometry.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[\\det\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\d&e\\end{pmatrix}=ae-bd\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>“No unique solution” is not the same as “no solution.”</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Classifying unique, inconsistent and dependent systems\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Classifying unique, inconsistent and dependent systems</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">line relation</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">equation evidence</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">solution count</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">different gradients</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">det≠0</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">one</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">same gradient, different intercept</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">contradiction</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">none</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">same line</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">repeated equation</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">infinitely many</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">classification requires structure, not only a solver warning</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 3 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Worked example · singular systems need a second question","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Classify \\(2x+4y=6\\) with (i) \\(x+2y=4\\) and (ii) \\(x+2y=3\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Compare coefficients</span><p>Both second equations have left side equal to half the first left side.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Case i</span><p>Half of 6 is 3, not 4, so the equations contradict: no solution.</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Case ii</span><p>Half of the entire first equation is \\(x+2y=3\\), so the equations coincide: infinitely many solutions.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">The same zero determinant can correspond to two different solution types.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Classify \\(3x-6y=9\\) with \\(x-2y=3\\), and explain the graph.</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-3\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>The first equation is three times the second, so the lines coincide and every point on the line is a solution.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>All coefficients and the constant scale by the same factor. Therefore the equations encode identical information.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">What additional evidence distinguishes “parallel” from “coincident” after a solver reports singularity?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 3","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Determinant zero means no solution.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>It means no unique solution; the system may have none or infinitely many.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Compare the augmented constants or inspect the graphs after detecting singularity.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>What does \\(0=5\\) after elimination mean? What does \\(0=0\\) mean?</p><p><b>Check:</b> \\(0=5\\): inconsistent, no solution. \\(0=0\\): dependent information, potentially infinitely many solutions.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Determining model parameters from conditions","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Each independent condition on a model creates an equation in its parameters. A quadratic has three coefficients, so three independent conditions can determine it. Technology solves the resulting system; substitution verifies the fitted model.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[A(t)=Pt^2+Qt+R\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\text{substitute each known }(t,A)\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>Parameters are unknown constants within a model; data conditions determine them.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Determining model parameters from conditions\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Determining model parameters from conditions</text>\n    <rect x=\"55\" y=\"82\" width=\"510\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#cfd8df\"/><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">condition</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.9\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">substitution</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"82\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#17324d\" opacity=\"0.96\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"106.75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\">equation</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">A(1)=9</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">P+Q+R</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"119.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"144.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">=9</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">A(2)=18</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">4P+2Q+R</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"157.0\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"182.125\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">=18</text><rect x=\"55.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">A(−1)=3</text><rect x=\"225.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"310.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">P−Q+R</text><rect x=\"395.0\" y=\"194.5\" width=\"170.0\" height=\"37.5\" fill=\"#edf8f7\" stroke=\"#d8e1e6\"/><text x=\"480.0\" y=\"219.625\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"750\" fill=\"#17324d\">=3</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">three independent conditions determine three parameters</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 4 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Worked example · fit a quadratic exactly","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>Given \\(A(1)=9\\), \\(A(2)=18\\), \\(A(-1)=3\\), find \\(P,Q,R\\).</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>Form the system</span><p>\\(P+Q+R=9\\), \\(4P+2Q+R=18\\), \\(P-Q+R=3\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Solve</span><p>\\(P=2,Q=3,R=4\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Write the model</span><p>\\(A(t)=2t^2+3t+4\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Verify</span><p>Substitute \\(t=1,2,-1\\) to recover 9, 18 and 3.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">A fitted formula is not accepted until it reproduces every supplied condition.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>Find \\(a,b,c\\) for \\(f(x)=ax^2+bx+c\\) through \\((0,2),(1,6),(2,14)\\).</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-4\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>\\(c=2\\), \\(a+b+c=6\\), \\(4a+2b+c=14\\). Solving gives \\(a=2,b=2,c=2\\), so \\(f(x)=2x^2+2x+2\\).</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>Use the point with \\(x=0\\) first because it immediately gives the constant term. Then solve the remaining two equations.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why might three conditions fail to determine three parameters uniquely?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 4","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“Any three equations determine three unknowns.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>The equations must be independent. Repeated or equivalent conditions do not add information.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Check rank, determinant or whether one equation is a combination of others.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>If the same data point is listed twice, does it provide a new independent condition?</p><p><b>Check:</b> No; it repeats the same equation.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Roots, intersections, residuals and contextual admissibility","html":"<div class=\"u1-concept\"><div><p>Technology can find roots of one equation or intersections of two models. Numerical outputs may be approximate, multiple or outside the contextual domain. Residuals quantify how closely a rounded solution satisfies the original equations.</p><div class=\"u1-formula\">\\[\\text{residual}=\\text{left side}-\\text{right side}\\]</div><div class=\"u1-language\"><b>Say it precisely</b><span>A small residual supports numerical accuracy; a contextual constraint decides admissibility.</span></div></div><svg class=\"concept-visual u1-v5-visual\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Roots, intersections, residuals and contextual admissibility\">\n    <rect width=\"620\" height=\"320\" rx=\"28\" fill=\"#f7f2eb\"/>\n    <rect x=\"16\" y=\"16\" width=\"588\" height=\"288\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#fffdf9\" stroke=\"#ded7ce\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">Roots, intersections, residuals and contextual admissibility</text>\n    <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"255\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#17324d\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"295\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"295\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"425\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"425\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"490\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"490\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"555\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><line x1=\"115\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"555\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#e5ded5\"/><path d=\"M120 220 C210 160 280 120 340 95 C425 60 485 80 545 135\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a1733\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"365\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><text x=\"380\" y=\"81\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#17324d\">root / intersection</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#5d6a75\">solver output must be checked for multiplicity, domain and context</text>\n    </svg></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Concept cycle 5 of 5"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Worked example · verify a rounded intersection","html":"<div class=\"u1-worked\"><div class=\"u1-problem\"><span>Problem</span><p>A GDC reports the intersection of two models as \\((1.364,-2.091)\\) for \\(7x-5y=20\\) and \\(3x+y=2\\). Verify using residuals.</p></div><div class=\"u1-steps\"><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>1</b><div><span>First residual</span><p>\\(7(1.364)-5(-2.091)-20\\approx0.003\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>2</b><div><span>Second residual</span><p>\\(3(1.364)+(-2.091)-2\\approx0.001\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>3</b><div><span>Interpret</span><p>Small nonzero residuals result from rounding the exact solution \\((15/11,-23/11)\\).</p></div></div><div class=\"u1-step\"><b>4</b><div><span>Conclude</span><p>The rounded point is consistent to about three decimal places.</p></div></div></div><div class=\"u1-conclusion\">Residuals distinguish rounding noise from a wrong solution.</div></div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Model the reasoning, not only the arithmetic"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Student turn","html":"<div class=\"student-turn u1-student\"><div class=\"u1-turn-head\"><b>Your turn</b><span>Reason before revealing</span></div><p>A model intersection gives time \\(t=-3.2\\) and \\(t=4.7\\), but the context begins at \\(t=0\\). Which solutions are admissible and why?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"turn-5\" aria-label=\"Student working\"></textarea><details><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Only \\(t=4.7\\) is admissible because the model domain excludes negative time. The negative algebraic root may still be mathematically valid.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Transfer the idea"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Explain the solution","html":"<div class=\"u1-solution\"><h3>Complete reasoning</h3><p>State both the mathematical solution set and the contextual restriction. Rejecting a root should be justified by the domain, not silently omitted.</p><div class=\"u1-explain-prompt\">Why can a graph window cause a solver user to miss a valid intersection?</div></div>","kind":"solution","eyebrow":"Make the hidden reasoning visible"},{"section":"Concept 5","title":"Misconception clinic","html":"<div class=\"u1-misconception\"><div class=\"u1-warning\">!</div><div><h3>Claim to diagnose</h3><blockquote>“The calculator displayed one root, so there is only one root.”</blockquote><h3>Why it fails</h3><p>A window, initial guess or solver mode may reveal only part of the solution set.</p><h3>Repair move</h3><p>Analyze the equation, inspect a suitable graph window and use multiple starting values when needed.</p><details><summary>Test the repair</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>What should be recorded with a graph-based solution in IB work?</p><p><b>Check:</b> A clear labelled sketch or technology evidence, the intersection/root values, and interpretation.</p></div></details></div></div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Diagnose · repair · verify"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Interactive investigation","html":"<div class=\"u1-lab-intro\"><div><h3>Predict first</h3><p>Change the coefficients of a 2×2 system. Predict when the determinant becomes zero and what that means for line intersections before calculating.</p><p class=\"u1-tech-note\">Use equation, matrix, graph or numerical-solver modes. Record the system, window or matrix, output and verification. A result is not complete until it is checked against equations and context.</p></div><div class=\"lab\" id=\"lesson-lab\" data-lab=\"1.8\"></div></div>","kind":"lab","eyebrow":"Technology must expose structure"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Transparent GDC protocol","html":"<div class=\"u1-protocol\"><article><b>1</b><h3>Define variables</h3><p>State units and what each unknown represents.</p></article><article><b>2</b><h3>Translate conditions</h3><p>Write one independent equation for each condition.</p></article><article><b>3</b><h3>Choose a method</h3><p>Graph for geometry, equation/matrix mode for efficient exact systems, numerical solver for nonlinear equations.</p></article><article><b>4</b><h3>Inspect output</h3><p>Determine uniqueness, accuracy and any warnings or multiple roots.</p></article><article><b>5</b><h3>Verify and interpret</h3><p>Substitute, compute residuals and apply contextual constraints.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-callout\"><b>IB communication:</b> record the model, relevant settings or entries, output, units and interpretation.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Use technology, but show mathematics"},{"section":"Synthesis","title":"Mixed reasoning challenge","html":"<div class=\"u1-synthesis\"><div class=\"u1-synthesis-grid\"><article><b>A</b><p>A quadratic model \\(A(t)=Pt^2+Qt+R\\) has values 9 at \\(t=1\\), 18 at \\(t=2\\) and 3 at \\(t=-1\\).</p></article><article><b>B</b><p>Form a 3×3 system for \\(P,Q,R\\) and solve it.</p></article><article><b>C</b><p>Verify all three data conditions and state the model.</p></article><article><b>D</b><p>Explain why three independent conditions are required for three parameters.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"synthesis\"></textarea><details><summary>Solution architecture</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>The equations are \\(P+Q+R=9\\), \\(4P+2Q+R=18\\), and \\(P-Q+R=3\\). Solving gives \\(P=2,Q=3,R=4\\), so \\(A(t)=2t^2+3t+4\\). Substitution produces 9, 18 and 3. Three independent conditions determine three unknown parameters; repeated information would not.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Connect representations and constraints"},{"section":"IB-style task","title":"Extended-response preview","html":"<div class=\"u1-task\"><div class=\"u1-task-meta\"><span>Paper 2 · system model</span><span>11 marks</span><span>GDC expected</span></div><h3>Campus event pricing</h3><p>Ticket bundles combine adult, student and child prices in three recorded sales totals.</p><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(a)</b><p>Define variables and write a 3×3 system.</p><span>[3]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(b)</b><p>Solve for the three prices.</p><span>[3]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(c)</b><p>Verify the totals.</p><span>[2]</span></div><div class=\"u1-task-part\"><b>(d)</b><p>Evaluate whether a negative or implausible price would be admissible.</p><span>[3]</span></div><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"exam\">Open all 3 IB tasks</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Read the command term and the context"},{"section":"Assessment literacy","title":"How marks are earned","html":"<div class=\"u1-command-grid\"><article><b>Form</b><p>Translate information into equations or a matrix.</p></article><article><b>Solve</b><p>Use an efficient method and report all admissible solutions.</p></article><article><b>Determine</b><p>Identify parameters or solution type.</p></article><article><b>Verify</b><p>Substitute into every original condition or compute residuals.</p></article><article><b>Interpret</b><p>State the contextual meaning with units.</p></article><article><b>Evaluate</b><p>Judge admissibility, independence and model limitations.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-mark-chain\"><span>model</span><i>→</i><span>method</span><i>→</i><span>accuracy</span><i>→</i><span>interpretation</span><i>→</i><span>evaluation</span></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Write for mathematical evidence"},{"section":"Exit ticket","title":"Three pieces of evidence","html":"<div class=\"u1-exit-wrap\"><div class=\"u1-exit\"><article><b>1</b><p>Solve \\(3x+2y=19\\), \\(x-y=1\\).</p></article><article><b>2</b><p>Classify \\(2x+4y=6\\), \\(x+2y=3\\).</p></article><article><b>3</b><p>Explain why a small residual is useful after a rounded numerical solution.</p></article></div><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"exit-ticket\"></textarea><details><summary>Teacher check</summary><div class=\"u1-answer\"><p>Expected \\((x,y)=(4.2,3.2)\\); the second system is dependent with infinitely many solutions; a residual measures how closely the rounded output satisfies the original equations.</p></div></details></div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Independent check"},{"section":"Reflection","title":"Where this idea travels next","html":"<div class=\"u1-connections\"><article><b>Functions</b><p>A system solution is an intersection of graphs.</p></article><article><b>Matrices</b><p>Coefficient matrices organize systems and expose determinant structure.</p></article><article><b>Regression</b><p>Parameter fitting generalizes the idea of using data conditions to determine a model.</p></article><article><b>Optimization and calculus</b><p>Later numerical solvers locate roots, intersections and critical conditions.</p></article></div><div class=\"u1-reflection\"><h3>Return to the inquiry</h3><p>Which parts of solving a system can be automated, and which require mathematical judgement about modelling, solution count and admissibility?</p><textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"reflection\"></textarea></div><div class=\"u1-route-launch\"><button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"practice\">Practice Studio</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"quiz\">Timed Quiz</button><button class=\"route-jump secondary-btn\" data-go=\"review\">Mastery evidence</button></div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Learn → practise → demonstrate → review"}]}};